\section*{Appendix B: Extremizing the Quantum Free Energy Yields the Schrödinger Equation}
Rewrite Eq.~\ref{eq:FQM} in terms of the probability amplitude \(\psi(x)\)
\begin{equation*}
F_{\text{quantum}}[\psi] = \int \left( U(x) |\psi(x)|^2 + \frac{\hbar^2}{2m} |\nabla \psi(x)|^2 \right) dx,
\end{equation*}
and extremize it subject to the normalization constraint \( \int |\psi(x)|^2 dx = 1 \) to obtain the quantum mechanical states. To enforce normalization, we introduce a Lagrange multiplier \( \lambda \) and define the Lagrangian density:
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{QM}} = U(x) |\psi(x)|^2 + \frac{\hbar^2}{2m} |\nabla \psi(x)|^2 - \lambda |\psi(x)|^2.
\end{equation*}

Applying the Euler–Lagrange equation
\(
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \psi^*} - \nabla \cdot \left( \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial (\nabla \psi^*)} \right) = 0,
\)
 we obtain
\begin{equation*}
(U(x) - \lambda)\psi(x) - \frac{\hbar^2}{2m} \nabla^2 \psi(x) = 0,
\end{equation*}
which rearranges to the time-independent Schrödinger equation
\begin{equation*}
\left( -\frac{\hbar^2}{2m} \nabla^2 + U(x) \right) \psi(x) = E \psi(x)
\end{equation*}
where we identify \( \lambda = E \) as the energy eigenvalue. Each eigenfunction \( \psi_n(x) \) corresponds to a stationary state of the system, and the global minimum of the free energy corresponds to the ground state.

The free energy functional in Eq.~\ref{eq:FQM} includes only the real part of the kinetic energy through the Fisher information of \( |\psi(x)| \). The full quantum mechanical energy includes an additional contribution from the phase structure of the wavefunction. Let \( \psi(x) = \sqrt{p(x)} e^{i\theta(x)} \). Then the expectation of the momentum-squared operator is:
\begin{equation*}
\langle \hat{P}^2 \rangle = \hbar^2 \left\langle |\nabla \log \psi(x)|^2 \right\rangle = \frac{\hbar^2}{4} J[p] + \hbar^2 \int p(x) \left( \nabla \theta(x) \right)^2 dx,
\end{equation*}
where \( J[p] = \int \frac{(\nabla p)^2}{p} dx \) is the Fisher information. The total average energy is:
\begin{equation*}
E_{\text{QM}} = \langle U \rangle + \frac{\hbar^2}{8m} J[p] + \frac{\hbar^2}{2m} \int p(x) \left( \nabla \theta(x) \right)^2 dx.
\end{equation*}
This shows that the Fisher-based free energy yields a lower bound on the total energy. The bound is tight when the wavefunction has constant phase (i.e., \( \nabla \theta(x) = 0 \)), in which case the additional term vanishes. In our framework, which optimizes over real-valued probability densities \( p(x) \) without explicit phase structure, the Schrödinger equation still emerges, but the full energy is only recovered in phase-symmetric cases.

\section*{Appendix C: Generalized Free Energy Extremization and Gaussian Solution}

Rewrite Eq.~\ref{eq:general_free_energy} in terms of the real-valued probability amplitude \(\psi(x) \) via \( p(x) = \psi(x)^2 \). To extremize \( F_\gamma[\psi] \), we introduce a Lagrange multiplier \( \lambda \) to enforce normalization. The Lagrangian density \(\mathcal{L}(\psi, \nabla \psi)\) becomes:
\begin{equation*}
 U(x) \psi^2 - k_B T f(\gamma) \psi^2 \log \psi^2 + g(\gamma) \cdot \frac{\hbar^2}{2m} |\nabla \psi|^2 - \lambda \psi^2.
\end{equation*}
Applying the Euler–Lagrange equation, we obtain:
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\left[ -g(\gamma) \cdot \frac{\hbar^2}{2m} \nabla^2 + U(x) + k_B T f(\gamma)  (1 + 2 \log \psi(x)) \right] \psi(x)  \nonumber \\
&&= \lambda \psi(x). \label{eq:generalShrodinger}
\end{eqnarray}
This is the generalized Schrödinger equation arising from extremizing the free energy, with eigenvalue \(\lambda \). The term with \(  \log \psi(x) \) introduces entropy-weighted nonlinearity, and \( \lambda \) is the eigenvalue associated with the extremum. Setting \( f(\gamma)=0\) and \( g(\gamma)=1\) recovers Shrödinger, and choosing \( f(\gamma)=1\) and \( g(\gamma)=0\) recovers Boltzmann.

To compute explicit results for energy and variance, we now assume that the extremal solution \( p(x) = \psi^2(x) \) is Gaussian with zero mean and variance \( \sigma^2 \). The free energy becomes:
\begin{equation*}
F(\sigma^2) = \frac{1}{2} m \omega^2 \sigma^2 - \frac{1}{2} k_B T f(\gamma) \log(2\pi e \sigma^2) + \frac{g(\gamma) \hbar^2}{8m \sigma^2}.
\end{equation*}
To find the extremum, we differentiate with respect to \(\sigma^2\) and solve the resulting quadratic equation to obtain:
\begin{equation*}
    \sigma^2 = f(\gamma)\frac{1}{2}\frac{k_B T}{m \omega^2} \left( 1 + \sqrt{ 1 + \frac{g(\gamma) \gamma^2}{f(\gamma)^2} } \right).
\end{equation*}
Substitution of this Gaussian solution into Eq.~\ref{eq:generalShrodinger} yields a free energy eigenvalue
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \lambda &=&  g(\gamma)\frac{\hbar^2}{4m\sigma^2} +f(\gamma)k_B T\left(1 - \frac{1}{2} \log\left(2 \pi \sigma^2\right)\right),
\end{eqnarray*}
which does not correspond to the physical energy. The eigenvalue \( \lambda \) in Eq.~\ref{eq:generalShrodinger} arises as a Lagrange multiplier enforcing the normalization constraint. While Eq.~\ref{eq:generalShrodinger} resembles a generalized Schrödinger equation, its eigenvalue lacks a direct thermodynamic interpretation. Instead, the physically meaningful energy is computed from the extremal distribution \( p(x) = \psi^2(x) \), using expectation values of the potential and (by equipartition) kinetic energy. In particular, the total oscillator energy \( \langle E \rangle = \langle U \rangle + \langle T \rangle \) is obtained from the variance of the optimal Gaussian. This distinction is analogous to classical thermodynamics, where the minimum of a free energy functional determines equilibrium properties, but does not itself represent the internal energy.

For completeness, we confirm that the Gaussian ansatz indeed satisfies Eq.~\ref{eq:generalShrodinger} by substituting
\(
\psi(x) = \left(2\pi \sigma^2\right)^{-1/4} \exp\left(-\frac{x^2}{4\sigma^2}\right)
\)
into the left-hand side of Eq.~\ref{eq:generalShrodinger}, assuming a harmonic potential \( U(x) = \frac{1}{2}m\omega^2 x^2 \). This gives
\begin{eqnarray*}
& & \biggr[ g(\gamma) \frac{\hbar^2}{2m} \left(\frac{1}{2\sigma^2} - \frac{x^2}{4\sigma^4}\right)
+ \frac{1}{2}m\omega^2 x^2 
 \\
 & & + k_B T f(\gamma)\left(1 - \frac{x^2}{2\sigma^2} - \frac{1}{2} \log(2\pi\sigma^2)\right)\biggr]
 \psi(x)
= \lambda \psi(x),
\end{eqnarray*}
which is proportional to \( \psi(x) \) if and only if the coefficients of the \( x^2 \) and constant terms match on both sides. This occurs precisely when \( \sigma^2 \) is the extremal variance derived earlier, with \(\lambda\) the corresponding eigen value. Therefore, the extremal distribution not only minimizes the free energy but also satisfies the associated Euler–Lagrange equation, establishing the internal consistency of the variational solution.
