\subsection{Evolutionary Algorithm for In-Distribution Generalization}

Although many instruction datasets have been synthesized, most are based on simple strategies like Self-Instruct\citep{wang2023self}, offering limited incremental value. 
To achieve meaningful expansion, we design a three-step evolution process that targets both coverage and depth as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:evolution_process}.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{pic/evolution.png}
    \caption{Process of the evolution-based data synthesizing.}
    \label{fig:evolution_process}
\end{figure}
1. Metadata-Guided Random Evolution:
Based on the previously selected metadata, we adopt the Evol-Instruct method and randomly choose one of four dimensions—diversity, more reasoning steps, concretizing, or deepening, to guide each instruction’s evolution using State-of-the-Art advanced large model as synthesizer. This promotes diversity and avoids narrow expansion patterns.

2. Validation and Filtering:
Evolved instructions are evaluated by an advanced large model as an AI assistant to ensure instruction-following quality. Those that fail are filtered out, ensuring the final dataset remains clean and reliable.

3. Multi-Turn Dialogue Generation:
Each valid instruction is used to generate 1–4 rounds of dialogue, with the AI assistant simulating different roles. 
% This enhances instruction complexity and builds coherent multi-turn interactions for richer data.

