Since we are in the nonparametric regime, it is necessary to assume that the support of $Q_X$ is related to that of $P_X$; otherwise, as discussed in the previous paragraph, no information can be borrowed from the source domain, and the source samples offer no extra benefit.
In such cases, it is typically assumed that the support of the target domain lies within the source domain and, importantly, that both share the same intrinsic dimension. This alignment enables the use of density ratio techniques to effectively borrow information across domains.
\DM{Cite some papers that used density ratio to tackle covariate shift}. 
However, if the target domain lies on a lower-dimensional (approximate) manifold within the source domain, the density ratio approach is not applicable, as the target support becomes (approximately) singular with respect to the source support. 
Therefore, a more fine-grained analysis is necessary to characterize the optimal contribution of source samples in estimating $f^\star$ over the target domain, which is the key motivation of our work. 
