%%%%%%%%%%%%% Previous Intro %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%  

Despite its growing practical appeal, a comprehensive theoretical understanding of covariate shift settings is underexplored in the literature, particularly in large dimensions, where one expects geometric and smoothness constraints to help mitigate the curse of dimensionality. 
Although several attempts have been made to understand the effect of covariate shift on estimating $f^\star$, several theoretical gaps remain to be explored. In particular, our understanding of the interplay between the smoothness of the conditional mean function and the geometric relationship between the source and target domains (especially when the target domain lies near a submanifold of the source domain) is still limited. This paper takes a step toward bridging this gap.

Therefore, in this work, we study the pointwise estimation of $f^\star$ when it belongs to a general H\"{o}lder class of \emph{arbitrary smoothness} $\beta>0$, as opposed to the existing state of the art (see \cite{kpotufe2021marginal,pathak2022,kalavasis2024transfer}) which restrict to $\beta\in (0,1]$. We obtain the minimax rates in this setting which demonstrates the benefits of source data and the dampening of the curse of dimensionality in regions where $f^\star$ is highly regular.

Another key innovation of our work is the modeling of the target distribution \( Q \) as supported on an \emph{approximate manifold} as defined in \cref{def:appmanif} below (also see \cite{jiao2023deep,lee2006riemannian,meilua2024manifold,bickel2007local}). 
Unlike idealized settings where the target data is assumed to lie exactly on a low-dimensional submanifold, real-world data often arise from noisy embeddings or perturbations that render such assumptions unrealistic. We adopt a flexible framework where the target domain is allowed to deviate from  a $d$-dimensional smooth manifold embedded in $\R^D$ ($D\gg d$). Interestingly, we show that the minimax rates in this setting exhibit a phase transition with respect to the ``distance" between the target domain and the manifold. 





