\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Half a century ago, Black and Scholes unveiled their revolutionary methodology
for the valuation and hedging of options in equity markets through their seminal
work~\cite{BS73}. In a serendipitous coincidence, the \emph{Chicago Board
  Options Exchange} inaugurated its operations in April 1973, a month prior to
Black and Scholes' publication, and it has evolved to become the world's
preeminent options exchange by volume. Today, a large number of options written
on various underlyings are well-established and liquidly traded financial
products. This growth in prevalence is to a large extent attributable to an
enhanced comprehension of the mathematics of options, pioneered by Black and
Scholes' work. While the Black-Scholes model and its induced pricing formula are
still foundational for derivatives pricing, their role has shifted to serving
primarily as a benchmark or quotation model, such as in the calculation of implied
volatilities. It is well-acknowledged among practitioners and academics alike
that the Black-Scholes model has its limitations, most notably its assumption of
constant volatility, which is at odds with empirical evidence indicating that
volatility is both time-varying and stochastic. Subsequent to the influential
contribution of Black and Scholes, a plethora of models have been introduced to
more accurately depict volatility's erratic behavior and enhance derivative
pricing. Among these are the stochastic volatility (SV) models of
Heston~\cite{Heston1993}, the \emph{SABR model} by Hagan et al.~\cite{HKLW02},
the Barndorff--Nielsen and Shephard model~\cite{BNS02}, and Dupire's local
volatility models~\cite{Dupire1994}. The latest major innovation in this field
is the advent of \emph{rough SV models}, which were more recently put forward by
Gatheral et al.~\cite{GJR18}. Rough SV models fit the volatility surface
effectively with few parameters, and provide more realistic volatility
time-series than classical SV models.\par

A further advancement in the field of stochastic volatility modeling is their
extension to a multivariate framework, capturing the dynamics of multiple asset
prices and their finite-rank covariance structure simultaneously. Multivariate
stochastic volatility models, also called \emph{stochastic covariance models},
are essential tools for managing the risk of asset portfolios, as well as for
pricing and hedging portfolios of derivatives or instruments written on multiple
underlyings such as spread or basket options. Classical approaches for modeling
the instantaneous covariance process in the multivariate case include
matrix-valued pure-jump models based on positive semi-definite
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck processes~\cite{BNS13, MKPS12, BK23}, pure diffusion-based
models like those based on Wishart processes~\cite{DFGT07, GS10}, and hybrids of
the former two classes, namely affine jump-diffusions on positive semi-definite
matrices~\cite{LT08, CFMT11}.\par{}

In this paper, we investigate the next evolutionary stage in stochastic
volatility modeling: function-valued infinite-rank stochastic volatility models
of affine type that emerge as limits of multivariate affine jump-diffusion SV
models as the number of independent risk factors increase to infinity.\par{}

Function-valued models play a crucial role in finance, especially in modeling
the dynamics of forward rates and forward price curves in fixed-income and
commodity markets. The key idea is to directly model the arbitrage-free forward
prices for all maturity dates simultaneously, rather than using a parametric
model for the underlying spot asset and then deriving the forward prices through
a spot-forward relation. This direct modeling approach was initially applied in
interest rate markets by Heath, Jarrow, and Morton~\cite{HJM92}, hence it is
referred to as the HJM approach. Since then, this methodology has been extended
to various other markets, including commodities~\cite{BBK08} and stock
options~\cite{KP15}.\par{}

In commodity markets, the HJM modeling approach is particularly advantageous in
energy and weather related markets where establishing a valid relation between
spot and forward prices is challenging. Indeed, in these markets the
conventional buy-and-hold hedging strategy for commodity forwards may be
impractical due to limitations such as non- or limited storability (as in the
case of electricity and gas, see e.g., \cite{BBK08}) or the underlying assets
being simply non-tradable (as for example wind speed or temperature indexes, see
e.g., \cite{BSB12}). However, futures, forwards, and other derivatives written
on day-ahead power prices or index-based underlyings are sometimes liquidly
traded. For instance, power futures from day-ahead to, in some areas, 3-year
delivery periods are liquid, and renewable energy forward production curves can
be tracked with reasonable high frequency given a flow of weather
predictions. In these markets, the focus therefore shifts to directly modeling
the forward dynamics following the HJM modeling paradigm.\par{}

The limitations in trading power or weather index-based underlyings not only
pose challenges in spot-forward modeling, but also contribute to high and
complex maturity-specific volatilities in the forward dynamics. These
complexities arise from factors such as intermittency, forward-looking
information, spatial and network risk, political decisions, and weather
forecasts, see also the discussion in~\cite[Section 1.3]{BenKru23}. The general
objective of this paper is to contribute to the mathematical formulation of a
framework to model these complex volatility structures within continuous-time
functional models, recently put forward in a number of papers~\cite{BRS18,
  BS18,BS24, CKK22b, FK24}.\par{}

One key motivation of this line of research is to enhance the understanding of
the mathematics of options written on forward curves. Similar to the progression
from the ordinary Black-Scholes model to stochastic volatility models in the univariate
setting, we advocate for stochastic volatility models for functional forward
curve dynamics that: (1) can jointly capture the stylized facts of forward curve
dynamics and their implied or realized volatilities, and (2) are tractable
enough to efficiently price and hedge European-style options written on the
forwards.

\subsection{The Heath-Jarrow-Morton-Musiela Modeling Approach}
To present our main contributions and review the relevant literature, we begin
with a brief introduction to the Heath-Jarrow-Morton-Musiela (HJMM) type approach
we use for modeling forward curve dynamics in fixed-income or commodity
markets.\par{}

For any time $t\geq 0$ and maturity date $T\geq t$, we denote by $F(t,T)$ the
forward price at time $t$ maturing at time $T$. In other words, $F(t,T)$ is the
at time $t$ agreed-upon price for the purchase or sale of the underlying asset
at the future date $T$. By introducing the \emph{Musiela parametrization}
$x \equiv T-t$, we can express forward prices as functions of
the \emph{time-to-maturity}. Specifically, we define $f_t(x) \equiv F(t,t+x)$ in
case of an arithmetic model, or $f_{t}(x)\equiv \ln(F(t,t+x))$ in case of a
geometric model, where $x,t\geq 0$. The mapping $x \mapsto f_{t}(x)$ is then
referred to as the (logarithmic) \emph{forward price curve} at time $t\geq 0$.

Following the Heath-Jarrow-Morton-Musiela modeling framework~\cite{CT06} the
dynamics of the risk-neutral (logarithmic) forward prices can be described by a
stochastic partial differential equation of hyperbolic type given as:
\begin{align}\label{eq:HJMM}
  \begin{cases}
    \D f_{t}(x)&=\big(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}f_{t}(x)+g_{t}(x)\big)\D
  t+\sum_{i=1}^{d}\sigma_{t}^{(i)}(x)\D W_{t}^{(i)},\quad \forall t,x>0,\\
    ~~~f_{0}(x)&=F(0,x), \quad\forall x>0,    
  \end{cases}
\end{align}
where $\{(W^{(i)}_{t})_{t\geq 0}\colon i=1,\ldots,d\}$ is a family of
$d \in\mathbb{N} \cup\set{+\infty}$ independent real-valued Brownian
motions. The drift term $(g_{t}(x))_{x,t\geq 0}$ in the HJMM
equation~\eqref{eq:HJMM} either vanishes in the arithmetic case, or adheres to a
certain HJM no-arbitrage condition in the geometric case, see e.g.~\cite[Section
2.4]{CT06}, ensuring that the forward price processes $(F(t,T))_{0\leq t\leq T}$
are martingales for all maturities $T$.  

We refer to the diffusion coefficients
$\set{(\sigma_{t}^{(i)}(x))_{x,t\geq 0}\colon i=1,\ldots,d}$ in
equation~\eqref{eq:HJMM} as the \textit{instantaneous volatilities} of the
forward curve dynamics. The family of instantaneous
volatilities characterizes the covariance structure between forwards with
different time-to-maturity. The number $d\in\MN\cup\set{+\infty}$
represents the \emph{rank of the noise}, corresponding to the number of
independent risk factors driving the forward curve dynamics. If $d$ is finite we
refer to~\eqref{eq:HJMM} as a \emph{finite-rank HJM} model; otherwise it is called
an \emph{infinite-rank HJM} model.\par{}

\subsection{Stochastic Covariance Models for Forward Price Curve
  Dynamics}\label{sec:intro-stoch-covariance-models} 

Within the general framework of the HJMM model~\eqref{eq:HJMM}, we retain the
flexibility to model the instantaneous volatilities
$\sigma_{t}(x)$ and decide on the rank $d$. Similar to the univariate case, we can
distinguish between three subclasses of models:  

\begin{enumerate}
    \item In the \emph{Gauss-Markov HJM} class, $\sigma_{t}(x)$ is chosen as
      a deterministic function of $t$ and $x$, independent of the
      forward prices $(f_{t}(x))_{t, x \geq 0}$. This is the forward curve
      equivalent of a (time-dependent) Black-Scholes model. 

    \item In the \emph{Markovian HJM} class, we assume that $\sigma(\omega,t
      ,f_{t}(x)) = \sigma(t, f_{t}(x))$, meaning the randomness arises solely
      through the dependence on the forward price itself. This approach is
      analogous to the local volatility models in the univariate case. 

    \item In a \emph{stochastic volatility model}, we assume that
      $(\sigma_{t}(x))_{t, x \geq 0}$ is both stochastic and time-dependent,
      governed by a separate stochastic differential equation. 
\end{enumerate}

The modeling approach we adopt in this paper is the third type: a
function-valued stochastic volatility model. Due to its infinite-dimensional
nature, it is more appropriately termed a \emph{stochastic covariance
  model}. More precisely, we refer to the joint process
$(f_{t}, \sigma_{t}^{2})_{t \geq 0}$ as a stochastic covariance model for
forward cuve dynamics, where $(f_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ is given by~\eqref{eq:HJMM}
modulated by $(\sigma_{t})_{t \geq 0}$, which represents the square-root process
of some process $(\sigma^{2}_{t})_{\geq 0}$, to be specified in this
paper. These types of stochastic covariance models have been recently studied
in~\cite{BS18, BRS18, CKK22b, FK24, Kar22, BE24}.

\subsubsection{Non-Parametric Modeling of Forward Prices and Volatility}

In such function-valued stochastic covariance models, we interpret the HJMM
equation~\eqref{eq:HJMM} as a stochastic differential equation formulated within
a suitable space of forward curves (see Section~\ref{sec:forward-curve-dynamics}
below for details). This approach is non-parametric, as it leverages the entire
initial forward curve, represented by $x \mapsto f_{0}(x)$, along with a
\emph{instantaneous covariance process} $(\sigma_{t}^{2})_{t \geq 0}$, as
primary model inputs. In this function-valued framework, $\sigma_{t}^{2}$, for
$t \geq 0$, represents the covariance of $f_{t}$ across all time-to-maturities
and is defined as a positive, self-adjoint operator of rank $d$, acting on a
specific space of forward curves, and can be identified with some covariance
kernel on the time-to-maturity domain. This non-parametric stochastic modeling
has the advantage of capturing the continuous-time dynamics of the forward
curves while at the same time fitting well the realized and implied covariance
structure with only few hyperparameters. The initial forward curve
$x \mapsto f_{0}(x)$ can be extracted from market data using a smoothing method,
as detailed in~\cite[Section 1.6.3]{CT06}. Meanwhile, the volatility component
can be calibrated to option price data~\cite{BDL21}, estimated from realized
term structure data~\cite{BSV22a, BSV22b,
  schroers2024robustfunctionaldataanalysis} or fitted to both simultaneously.


\subsubsection{The Rank and Structure of Forward Curve Volatility: Empirics and Theory}

Empirical studies have shown that energy forwards exhibit idiosyncratic risk
across different maturities, characterized by high-dimensional noise and
distinctive stochastic correlation structures~\cite{Fre08,AKW10}. For instance,
a principal component analysis (PCA) of the \emph{NordPool} power futures market
conducted in~\cite{BBK08} revealed that more than ten risk factors are necessary
to explain 95\% of the variance. Similar results have been reported in other
studies, such as~\cite{Fre08} and~\cite{KO05}. These findings in energy markets
contrasts with the interest rate markets, where PCA indicates that only a small
number of risk factors is required to explain the variance~\cite{CT06}. These
empirical findings support the assumption of stochastic
volatility~\cite{schroers2024dynamicallyconsistentanalysisrealized}, but also
suggest the use of finite-rank HJM models. However, relying on the PCA analysis
alone can lead to suboptimal modeling decisions for pricing, hedging, and risk
management in forward markets due to the following theoretical and practical
considerations:

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Maturity-Specific Risk}: As observed in~\cite{CT06}, classical finite-rank HJM
  models are complete, allowing for perfect hedging of interest rate
  derivatives. This redundancy even allows to choose tenors of the hedging
  instruments that are independent of the derivative’s expiry
  date, leading to unrealistic hedging strategies. This issue can only be
  adequately addressed in the case of \emph{maturity-specific risk},
  where the volatility is modeled with infinite rank. 
\item \emph{Higher-Order Moments}: As argued by~\cite{Con01}, discarding
  risk factors based on a PCA in an a priori fashion can introduce biases when
  computing quantities that are nonlinear functions of the forward curve. This
  is particularly relevant for pricing and hedging forward curve derivatives
  with nonlinear payoffs, or for computing quantiles and
  value-at-risk. Therefore, for applications in pricing, hedging, and risk
  management, it is advisable to first model with maturity-specific
  risk, before analysing finite-rank approximations and their
  associated errors relative to the sound full-rank model. 

\item \emph{Covariance Representation}: Even for a moderate number of risk
  factors $d \in \mathbb{N}$, theere are $d \times (d-1)/2$ stochastic
  correlations to be computed, which poses a high-dimensional
  problem, especially in energy markets with up to ten identified risk factors. In the limit as $d \to \infty$,
  every covariance operator has a convenient representation as $L^{2}$-kernel, a
  function of time-to-maturity~\cite{BK14}. For modeling purposes, it is more
  practical to use a parametric family of kernels and their finite-rank
  approximations, with a low number of parameters, that provides good
  interpretability and expressiveness than working with a high-dimensional
  stochastic matrix.  
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Main Contributions and Related Literature}

This paper's primary contribution is the development of a novel stochastic
covariance model, $(f_{t}, \sigma^{2}_{t})_{t \geq 0}$, for forward curve
dynamics in fixed-income or commodity markets. The forward curve process,
$(f_{t})_{t \geq 0}$, is modeled using an infinite-rank HJMM
framework~\eqref{eq:HJMM}, while the covariance process,
$(\sigma_{t}^{2})_{t \geq 0}$, is represented as an affine process taking values
in the cone of positive trace-class operators. We show that this
model is numerically and analytically tractable, highly flexible, theoretically
robust, and effectively captures the characteristics and second-order moment
structure of forward curves.

\subsubsection{Affine Transform Formula and Finite-Rank Approximations}

The proposed stochastic covariance model $(f_{t}, \sigma^{2}_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ is
analytically tractable because it satisfies what is known as an \emph{affine
  transform formula}. Specifically, given a fixed initial forward curve $f_{0}$
and an initial instantaneous covariance operator $\sigma_{0}^{2}$, the
characteristic function of $(f_{t}, \sigma^{2}_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ at any time
$t \geq 0$ satisfies the following equation:
\begin{align}\label{eq:affine-transform-intro}
  \mathbb{E}\left[\E^{\langle f_{t}, u_{1}\rangle_{V} - \Tr(\sigma^{2}_{t} u_{2})}\right] = \E^{-\Phi(t,u) + \langle f_{0}, \psi_{1}(t,u)\rangle_{V} - \Tr(\sigma_{0}^{2} \psi_{2}(t,u))},
\end{align}
where $\Phi(\cdot, u_{1}, u_{2})$, $\psi_1(\cdot, u_{1}, u_{2})$, and
$\psi_2(\cdot, u_{1}, u_{2})$ are the unique solutions to an associated system
of \emph{generalized Riccati equations}. Here, $u_{1}$ is a function in a
Hilbert space of forward curves $(V,\langle \cdot,\cdot\rangle_{V})$, and
$u_{2}$ is a positive self-adjoint Hilbert-Schmidt operator on $V$. The affine
transform formula~\eqref{eq:affine-transform-intro} conveniently expresses the
characteristic function of the model $(f_{t}, \sigma_{t}^{2})_{t \geq 0}$ as an
exponential function of the initial values $(f_{0}, \sigma_{0}^{2})$ and
quasi-explicitly up to the solution of a system of associated deterministic
differential equations.\par{}

Below, in Theorem~\ref{thm:main-convergence}, we establish
the existence of a certain class of \emph{irregular affine processes} on the
cone of positive self-adjoint trace-class operators, where the drift is governed
by the Lyapunov operator of the Laplacian. We show in
Theorem~\ref{thm:heat-affine-model} that this operator-valued process is
well-suited for modeling the instantaneous covariance process of forward curve
dynamics and that the joint model, henceforth called the \emph{heat-modulated
  affine model}, satisfies the affine transform
formula~\eqref{eq:affine-transform-intro}.\par

The heat modulated affine model $(f_{t}, \sigma^{2}_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ is also numerically
tractable, as it allows for finite-rank approximations
$(f_{t}^{d}, \sigma_{t}^{2,d})_{t \geq 0}$, where
$(\sigma_{t}^{2,d})_{t \geq 0}$ denotes an affine process on positive operators
of rank $d$. Moreover, these finite-rank approximations satisfies a similar
affine transform formula to~\eqref{eq:affine-transform-intro}, with
$\Phi(\cdot, u_{1}, u_{2})$, $\psi_1(\cdot, u_{1}, u_{2})$, and
$\psi_2(\cdot, u_{1}, u_{2})$ replaced by their respective spectral Galerkin approximations
of rank $d$. This result is detailed in
Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-dim-approx-ScoV}, where we also provide explicit
convergence rates for the finite-rank approximation of affine stochastic
covariance models. Furthermore, we examine the robustness of pricing options
written on electricity forward curves with respect to these finite-rank
approximations in Corollary~\ref{cor:robustness}. General robustness results for approximations of the
instantaneous covariance in Hilbert space-valued stochastic covariance models were
discussed in~\cite{BE24}.\par{}

The proofs of our main results are adapted from the work
in~\cite{karbach2023finiterank} and extended to the setting of \emph{irregular
  affine processes}, i.e. affine processes with an unbounded drift
operator. Affine stochastic volatility models in Hilbert spaces were initially
introduced in~\cite{CKK22b}, with the subclass of Hilbert-valued
Barndorff--Nielsen-Shepard models presented in~\cite{BRS18, BS24}. The affine class has been shown to be highly flexible,
particularly in the volatility component, allowing for L\'evy noise as well as
state-dependent and drifted jump dynamics of infinite variation. Finite-rank
approximations of affine processes were explored
in~\cite{karbach2023finiterank}, and the long-term behavior of affine stochastic
covariance models in Hilbert spaces was investigated in~\cite{FK24}. A Hilbert
space-valued extension of the Heston stochastic volatility model was introduced
in~\cite{BS18}, and \emph{a heat modulated version} was examined
in~\cite{BLDP22}. This article can be considered as an analogous heat-modulation
extension of the affine class in~\cite{CKK22b}. 

\subsubsection{Regularization by Heat Modulation}\label{sec:heat-modulation}

The main idea behind the heat modulation of stochastic covariance models for
forward curve dynamics is to use the instantaneous covariance process
$(\sigma^{2}_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ to \emph{regularize the noise} in the HJMM
equation~\eqref{eq:HJMM}. This is accomplished by augmenting the drift
component of the instantaneous covariance process with the Lyapunov operator of
the Laplacian. The Lyapunov operator is defined as
$L_{\Delta}(\cdot) \coloneqq \Delta \cdot + \cdot \Delta$, where $\Delta$
represents the Laplacian acting on an appropriate forward curve space. The
resulting heat-modulated stochastic covariance models derive their name from
this connection to the Laplacian, which is know to generate the so-called heat
semigroup $(T_{t})_{t \geq 0}$. The heat semigroup is a uniformly exponentially
stable, analytic semigroup of bounded, self-adjoint linear
operators and incorporating the heat semigroup into the stochastic volatility
component, or \emph{heat modulation}, brings several beneficial effects to the models,
which will be explored in this paper. Below, we discuss the two most prominent
effects that motivate the use of heat modulation in forward curve modeling.\par{}

First, as proposed in~\cite{Con01} and~\cite{Dou14}, the noise in forward curve
dynamics is best modeled by infinite-rank cylindrical Brownian or L\'evy
noise. Using cylindrical noise, rather than, for example, a $\cQ$-Brownian
motion with some covariance operator $\cQ$, has the advantage that all
information about the covariance is encoded solely in the operator
$\sigma_{t}^{2}$ and is not mixed with the contributions of $\cQ$, which are
indistinguishable from each other in the data. In particular, this approach
allows the instantaneous volatilities $\sigma_{t}^{(i)}$ in~\eqref{eq:HJMM} to
be interpreted as the principal components of $\sigma_{t} \D W_{t}$ for any
$t \geq 0$, which can be effectively fitted to data, as outlined
in~\cite[Section 1.7]{CT06}. Additionally, since cylindrical Brownian noise
relates to a Brownian motion with the identity as its covariance operator, it
eliminates the need for cumbersome assumptions about the commutativity of
instantaneous covariance operators and the covariance $\cQ$, as
required in~\cite[Assumption 2.11]{CKK22b} and~\cite[Proposition
3.2]{BRS18}. Therefore, the cylindrical setting is both more natural and more
convenient for covariance modeling in forward curve spaces.

However, for the HJMM equation with cylindrical noise to be well-posed as an SDE
in a suitable Hilbert space, the instantaneous volatility process must be
Hilbert-Schmidt operator-valued or equivalently, the instantaneous covariance
operators must be of trace-class, see~\cite{GM11}. The latter space is only
Banach, and proving the existence of, e.g., affine processes on this space is
challenging because standard stochastic analysis tools for Hilbert spaces are
not readily applicable. We demonstrate that using the Lyapunov operator of the
Laplacian in the drift of an affine process allows us to work within Hilbert
spaces, specifically on a Gelfand triple of Hilbert-Schmidt operators. By
applying a regularization result from~\cite{Tem69}, we show that an any affine
process driven by the Lyapunov operator of the Laplacian is indeed trace-class
operator-valued, and is therefore well-suited for the cylindrical noise setting.

Secondly, from a modeling perspective, it is unrealistic to assume that the
noise, cylindrical or not, takes values in the state-space of smooth forward
curves. Indeed, forward curves are constructed by
smoothing forward rates or prices using some fitting methods, as outlined
in~\cite[Section 1.2]{Fil01}. The noise itself is not observed directly, can
therefore not be smoothed from data and should not be assumed
to have the same level of smoothness as the forward curves themselve. Accordingly, the
instantaneous volatility operators must \emph{regularize} the less
smooth forward curve noise effectively. We show that, similar to the first
regularization property at the operator level, the heat semigroup also yields a
regularization at the function space level, smoothing the noise and
ensures weak differentiability of the resulting forward curves. This type of
regularization has also been discussed in~\cite{BLDP22} for the
infinite-dimensional Heston stochastic volatility model.

\section{Stochastic Volatility Modulated HJMM and Affine
  Processes}\label{sec:stoch-covar-model}

In this section, we introduce the heat-modulated affine stochastic volatility
model. A stochastic volatility model comprises two key components: the price
process and the instantaneous (co)variance process. We begin by addressing these
components separately. In Section~\ref{sec:forward-curve-dynamics}, we examine a
generic stochastic volatility-modulated HJMM model to describe the
spatio-temporal dynamics of logarithmic forward curves in fixed-income or
commodity markets. Subsequently, in
Section~\ref{sec:admissible-irregular-affine-processes}, we present a particular
class of operator-valued affine processes designed to model the instantaneous
covariance process associated with the forward price curve dynamics.

\subsection{The Dynamics of Forward Curves}\label{sec:forward-curve-dynamics}

To interpret the HJMM stochastic partial differential equation~\eqref{eq:HJMM},
we view it as a stochastic differential equation taking values in some Hilbert
spaces of viable forward curves. In the following two paragraphs, we recall
the definition and a few essential properties of a class of forward curve spaces
introduced in~\cite[Chapter 4 \& 5]{Fil01} and~\cite{Con01, Dou14}, and subsequently
give sufficient conditions for the existence of mild solutions to the HJMM equation.

\subsubsection{The State-Spaces}\label{sec:the-state-space}

Let us denote by $[0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}]$ the interval of all
time-to-maturities with $\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}\leq \infty$ the maximal
time-to-maturity of all available forwards in a market. In an idealized setting, one
could assume $\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}=\infty$.  The main empirical and economic
features of any forward curve $[0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}]\ni x\mapsto f_{t}(x)$
include a certain level of smoothness with respect to time-to-maturity, the
existence of a limit at the long end of the curve (often referred to as the
\emph{long-rate} or \emph{long-term price}), and a characteristic flattening of the curve for
large time-to-maturities, see the discussion in~\cite{Con01,Dou14},
and~\cite[Section 1.2]{Fil01} for the construction of forward curves through the
smoothing of market data.\par{}

The following space was proposed in~\cite{Fil01} as state-space for the HJMM
equation in fixed-income markets, and its adequacy for forward curve modeling in
commodity markets was further discussed in~\cite{BK14}: Let $\beta > 0$ and
denote by $H_{\beta}$ the space of all absolutely 
continuous functions $f \colon \MRplus \to \mathbb{R}$ such that
\begin{align*}
  \norm{f}_{\beta} \coloneqq \left( |f(0)|^{2} + \int_{\mathbb{R}_{+}} \E^{\beta
  x} |f'(x)|^{2} \, \mathrm{d}x \right)^{1/2} < \infty,   
\end{align*} 
where $f'$ denotes the weak derivative of $f$. We equip $H_{\beta}$
with the inner product 
\begin{align*}
  \langle f, g \rangle_{\beta} \coloneqq f(0) g(0) + \int_{\mathbb{R}_{+}}
  \E^{\beta x} f'(x) g'(x) \, \mathrm{d}x, \quad \text{for } f, g \in
  H_{\beta}.  
\end{align*}
Irregularities in the forward curve are penalized by the weight function
$x\mapsto\E^{\beta x}$ and the flattening of the curve is thus expressed through
the penalization of the weak derivative $f'$. The existence of the long-rate or
long-term price is ensured by appending the value $f(0)$. In practice, when the
domain is bounded, the existence of the long-term price is immediate and we use a
weighted Sobolev space $H_{0}^{1}(0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}})$, with
measure $\E^{\beta x}\D x$, as state-space, see~\cite{Con01}. It is worth noting that
$(H_{\beta}, \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_{\beta})$ is a separable Hilbert
space, which is compactly embedded $H_{\beta}\hookrightarrow L^{2}(\mathbb{R}_{+}, \E^{\gamma x}
\mathrm{d}x)\oplus \mathbb{R}$ whenever $0 < \gamma < \beta$, see \cite{Tap13},
whereas $H_{0}^{1}(0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}})$ is compactly embedded in
$H_{0}^{1}(0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}})\hookrightarrow L^{2}(0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}})$ by Rellich's embedding
theorem~\cite{Rel30}. The Hilbert space $(V,\langle \cdot,\cdot\rangle_{V})$, with
either $V=H_{\beta}$ or $V=H_{0}^{1}(0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}})$, has further
convenient properties: For $x \in \mathbb{R}_{+}$, the point evaluation map
$\delta_{x} \colon V \to \mathbb{R}$ is a continuous linear functional
on $V$, see e.g, \cite[Theorem 2.1]{FTT10}. Thus, for any $x \in
[0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}]$ we can identify $\delta_{x}$ with an
element $u_{x} \in V$ such that $\langle f, u_{x} \rangle_{V} =
\delta_{x}(f)$. Moreover, the left-shift semigroup $(S(t))_{t \geq 0}$, given by
$S(t) f(x) = f(x + t)$ for $0\leq x\leq \Theta_{\mathrm{max}}-t$ and
$S(t)f(x)=0$ for $x\geq \Theta_{\mathrm{max}}-t$, is strongly continuous on $V$,
with its infinitesimal generator being the spatial differentiation operator
$\frac{\partial}{\partial x}$, see~\cite[Section 2.3]{farkas2015isem}. 

\subsubsection{The Stochastic Volatility Modulated HJMM
  Equation}\label{sec:HJMM-equation} 

Throughout this paper, we denote by $(V, \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_V)$ and
$(H, \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_H)$ two separable Hilbert spaces, such that
$V \hookrightarrow H \hookrightarrow V^*$, where $V^*$ is the adjoint space with
respect to $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_H$. We interpret $V$ as a space of
\emph{regular} forward curves and $H$ as a space of \emph{less regular}
curves. In practice, this could represent, for example, the pair
$(H_0^1(0, \theta_{\mathrm{max}}), \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_{H_0^1})$ and
$(L^2(0, \theta_{\mathrm{max}}), \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_{L^2})$, or, the pair $(H_\beta, \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_\beta)$ and
$(L^2(\mathbb{R}_+, \E^{\gamma x} \mathrm{d}x) \oplus \mathbb{R}, \langle \cdot,
\cdot \rangle_\gamma)$, with $\langle f, g \rangle_\gamma \coloneqq f(0)g(0) + \int_{\mathbb{R}_+} \E^{\gamma x} f(x) g(x) \, \D x$,
for $\gamma < \beta$. 

Both spaces $H$ and $V$ can serve as state spaces for the HJMM equation, depending on
modeling preferences and the desired smoothness of forward curves in the
respective markets. Here, to interpret the HJMM equation~\eqref{eq:HJMM}, we treat it
as a stochastic differential equation (SDE) in the space of \emph{smooth forward
curves} $V$:
\begin{align}\label{eq:HJMM-SDE}
\begin{cases}
    \D f_t = \big(\mathcal{A} f_t + g_t\big) \D t + \sigma_t \D W_t, \\
    f_0 = F(0, \cdot) \in V,
\end{cases}  
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{A} \coloneqq \frac{\partial}{\partial x}$, $(g_t)_{t \geq 0}$
denotes the $V$-valued drift, $(W_t)_{t \geq 0}$ is a cylindrical Brownian
motion on $H$, and $(\sigma_t)_{t \geq 0}$ represents the \emph{instantaneous
  volatility process} of $(f_t)_{t \geq 0}$. The initial value
$x \mapsto f_0(x) = F(0, x)$ is referred to as the \emph{initial forward
  curve}.\par{}

Note that the noise $(W_t)_{t \geq 0}$ is not modeled as a $V$-valued Brownian
motion, and its values are generally less regular in terms of time-to-maturity
compared to the forward curves $x \mapsto f_t(x) \in V$ at any given time
$t \geq 0$. This is consistent with the observation that forward curves are
typically constructed using smoothing techniques from market data, whereas the
noise is not subject to such smoothing. In order for $(f_t)_{t \geq 0}$ to
remain in the space $V$, the instantaneous volatility process
$(\sigma_t)_{t \geq 0}$ must necessarily map from $H$ into $V$.

Furthermore, for every $t \geq 0$ and $h \in H$, the instantaneous covariance
operator $\sigma_t$ can be represented as:
\begin{align}\label{eq:covariance-mapping}
  \sigma_t f = \sum_{i=1}^{d} \langle v_i, f \rangle_H \sigma^{(i)}_t, \quad \text{for } f \in H,
\end{align}
where $(v_i)_{i \in \mathbb{N}}$ is an orthonormal basis (ONB) of $H$, and
$(\sigma^{(i)}_t)_{i \in \mathbb{N}} \subseteq V$ is a sequence of
\emph{instantaneous volatilities} as in~\eqref{eq:HJMM}. If $d = \infty$, we
implicitly assume that the series in \eqref{eq:covariance-mapping} converges in
$V$; this and the form in~\eqref{eq:covariance-mapping} will be ensured by the
general Assumption~\ref{assump:integration} below. 


At this level of generality, we have not yet imposed any model assumption on the
process $(\sigma_{t})_{t \geq 0}$. However, in order to solve
equation~\eqref{eq:HJMM-SDE}, we need to impose certain
integrability conditions on $(\sigma_{t})_{t \geq 0}$. Indeed,
following~\cite[Section 3.10]{GM11}, a mild solution to~\eqref{eq:HJMM-SDE} is
given by the variation-of-constant formula
\begin{align}\label{eq:solution-HJMM}
  f_{t} = S(t)f_{0} + \int_{0}^{t} S(t-s)\sigma_{s} \D W_{s}, \quad t \geq 0,
\end{align}
given that the stochastic integral $\int_{0}^{t} S(t-s)\sigma_{s} \D W_{s}$ is
well-defined. This is the case whenever the process $(S(t-s)\sigma_s)_{s \leq
  t}$ is progressively measurable and satisfies
\begin{align}\label{eq:integral-condition}
\mathbb{E} \left[\int_0^t
  \norm{S(t-s)\sigma_s}^{2}_{\mathcal{L}_2(H,V)} \D s \right] <
  \infty,\quad \forall t\geq 0. 
\end{align}
Here, $\mathcal{L}_{2}(H,V)$ denotes the space of
Hilbert-Schmidt operators from $H$ to $V$. Equipped with the
inner product $\langle \cdot, \cdot
\rangle_{\mathcal{L}_{2}(H,V)}$, given by  
\begin{align}\label{eq:inner-product-HS}
  \langle A, B \rangle_{\mathcal{L}_2(H,V)} \coloneqq
  \mathrm{Tr}(B^{*}A) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \langle A e_n, B e_n
  \rangle_{V}, \quad  A, B \in \mathcal{L}_{2}(H,V).
\end{align}
The space
$(\mathcal{L}_{2}(H,V),\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_{\mathcal{L}_{2}(H,V)})$ is
a separable Hilbert space, where we denote the \emph{adjoint} of $A\in\cL(H,V)$
by $A^{*}$, and the definition of the inner-product~\eqref{eq:inner-product-HS}
is independent of the choice of the basis $(e_{i})_{i\in\MN}\subseteq H$. The
shift semigroup $(S(t))_{t\geq 0}$ is strongly continuous on $V$, so that
$(S(t-s)\sigma_s)_{s \leq t}$ is progressively measurable whenever the process
$(\sigma_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ is. In order for
condition~\eqref{eq:integral-condition} to be satisfied,
$(\sigma_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ must already take values in $\mathcal{L}_{2}(H,V)$, as
$(S(t))_{t\geq 0}$ is a semigroup of merely bounded operators on $V$ and
$\norm{S(t-s)\sigma_{s}}_{\mathcal{L}_{2}(H,V)}\leq
\norm{S(t-s)}_{\cL(V)}\norm{\sigma_{s}}_{\mathcal{L}_{2}(H,V)}$. Consequently,
if the noise $(W_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ is a cylindrical Brownian motion on $H$ any
viable instantaneous volatility process $(\sigma_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ in the
model~\eqref{eq:HJMM-SDE} satisfy the following assumption: 

\begin{assumption}{A}\label{assump:integration}
  The instantaneous volatility process $(\sigma_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ is progressively
  measurable with respect to some filtration $(\cF_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ to which
  $(W_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ is adapted, and satisfies $\sigma_{t}\in
  \cL_{2}(H,V)$ for all $t\geq 0$ and~\eqref{eq:integral-condition}.
\end{assumption}

\begin{remark}
  If the modeling of the forward curves takes place in the space $H$ rather than
  $V$, with $(W_t)_{t \geq 0}$ still being a cylindrical Brownian motion on $H$,
  we can replace $V$ with $H$ in Assumption~\ref{assump:integration} and still
  obtain an assumption leading to viable instantaneous volatility
  processes. Moreover, note that a cylindrical Brownian motion on $H$ is not an
  $H$-valued stochastic process, but can be identified with a Brownian motion on
  some larger space $G$, see~\cite{PZ07}. This implies that, even in this case,
  the noise remains less regular in time-to-maturity than the forward curves. 
\end{remark}

\subsection{The affine instantaneous covariance
  process}\label{sec:admissible-irregular-affine-processes} 

In this section, we introduce a class of operator-valued stochastic processes
that we propose as the instantaneous volatility process
$(\sigma_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ in equation~\eqref{eq:HJMM-SDE}. Following the
previous section, this process should satsify
Assumption~\ref{assump:integration} above.\par{}

We set $\cH \df \cL_{2}(H,H)$, $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle\df \langle \cdot,\cdot
\rangle_{_{\mathcal{L}_2(H,H)}}$ and $\norm{\cdot}=\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle^{1/2}$. Let $\cH^{+}$ be the set of all
positive operators in $\cH$, i.e.,
$\cH^{+} \df \{ A \in \cH \colon \langle A v, v \rangle_{H} \geq 0 \text{ for
  all } v \in H \}$. Note that $\cH^{+}$ is closed and a convex cone in $\cH$,
meaning that $\cH^{+} + \cH^{+} \subseteq \cH^{+}$,
$\lambda \cH^{+} \subseteq \cH^{+}$ for all $\lambda \geq 0$,
$ \cH^{+}\cap (-\cH^{+}) = \{ 0 \}$. The cone $\cH^{+}$ induces a partial
ordering $\leq_{\cH^{+}}$ on $\cH$, defined by $x \leq_{\cH^{+}} y$ whenever
$y - x \in \cH^{+}$. Lastly, define $\cV\df \cL_{2}(V^{*},H)\cap\cL_{2}(H,V)$
with $V^{*}$ the adjoint of $V$ with respect to
$\langle \cdot,\cdot\rangle_{H}$.

\begin{definition}\label{def:admissible-irregular}
  Let  $\chi \colon \cH \to \cH$ be given by
\(\chi(\xi) = \xi \one_{\norm{\xi} \leq 1}(\xi)\). We call the tuple
$(b,\mathbf{B},m,\mu)$ an \textit{admissible parameter set} (with respect to
$\chi$) if the following holds:  
  \begin{defenum}
    \item\label{item:m-2moment} a measure
    $m\colon\cB(\cHpluso)\to [0,\infty]$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] $\int_{\cHpluso} \| \xi \|^2 \,\dm < \infty$ and
    \item[(b)] $\int_{\cHpluso}|\langle\chi(\xi),h\rangle|\,\dm<\infty$ for all
      $h\in\cH$   
   and there exists an element $I_{m}\in \cH$ such that $\langle
   I_{m},h\rangle=\int_{\cHpluso}\langle \chi(\xi),h\rangle\, m(\D\xi)$ for
   every $h\in\cH$;  
    \end{enumerate}   
  \item \label{item:drift} a vector $b\in\cH$ such that
    \begin{align*}
      \langle b, v\rangle - \int_{\cHpluso} \langle
      \chi(\xi), v\rangle \,m(\D\xi) \geq 0\, \quad\text{for all}\;v\in\cHplus;
    \end{align*}    
  \item \label{item:affine-kernel} a $\cHplus$-valued measure $\mu \colon
    \mathcal{B}(\cHpluso) \rightarrow \cHplus$ such that the kernel 
    $M(x,\D\xi)$, for every $x\in\cHplus$ defined on $\mathcal{B}(\cHpluso)$ by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:affine-kernel-M}
      M(x,\D\xi)\df \frac{\langle x, \mu(\D\xi)\rangle }{\norm{\xi}^{2}},
    \end{align}
    satisfies
    \begin{align}\label{eq:affine-kernel-quasi-mono}
      \int_{\cHplus\setminus \{0\}} \langle \chi(\xi), u\rangle\,M(x,\D\xi)< \infty,  
    \end{align}
    for all $u,x\in \cHplus$ such that $\langle u,x \rangle = 0$;
  \item\label{item:linear-operator-unbounded}
    $\mathbf{B}\in\cL(\cV,\cV^{*})$  is of the form
    \begin{align*}
      \mathbf{B}(x)=Bx+xB^{*}+\Gamma(x),\quad x\in \cV,  
    \end{align*}
    where:
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] $B\colon\dom(B)\subseteq H\to H$ is a linear and
      densely defined operator with $B,B^{*}\in\cL(V,V^{*})$ 
      and such that there exists a complete ONB $(e_{i})_{i\in\MN}$ in
      $H$ of eigenvectors that satisfy
      \begin{align*} 
        B e_{i}=-\lambda_{i} \be_{i}  
      \end{align*}
      for a sequence $(\lambda_{i})_{i\in\MN}\subseteq\MRplus$ with
      \begin{align*}
        0\leq \lambda_{1}\leq \lambda_{2}\leq \ldots \leq \lambda_{n}\to \infty
        \quad\text{as } n\to\infty.  
      \end{align*}
    \item[(b)] $\Gamma\colon \cH\to \cH$ is a bounded linear operator such
      that $\Gamma(\cHplus)\subseteq\cHplus$ and  
      \begin{align*}
        \left\langle \Gamma(x), u \right\rangle 
        - 
        \int_{\cHpluso}
        \langle \chi(\xi),u\rangle 
        \frac{\langle \mu(\D\xi), x \rangle}{\| \xi\|^2 }
        \geq 0,
      \end{align*}
      for all $x,u \in \cHplus$ such that $\langle u,x\rangle=0$.
    \item[(c)] Moreover, we assume that the operator $B$ satisfies the
      following coercivity assumption: there exist $\alpha_{0},\alpha_{1}>0$
      and $\lambda\geq 0$ such that
      \begin{align*}
        \qquad\qquad\alpha_{0}\norm{u}^{2}_{V}\leq 2 
        \langle B u,u\rangle_{H}+\lambda_{0}\norm{u}_{H}^{2}\leq
        \alpha_{1}\norm{u}_{V}^{2}\quad\text{for all } u\in V,
      \end{align*}
      where $\langle B u,u \rangle_{H}=B(u)(u)$ denotes the linear map
      $B(u)\in V^{*}$ applied to $u\in V$.  
    \end{enumerate}
  \end{defenum} 
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Laplacian and the heat semigroup]\label{ex:Laplacian}
The main example of an operator $B$ that
satisfies~\cref{item:linear-operator-unbounded} is the Laplacian, $\Delta$. For
this, let $H = L^{2}(0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}, \E^{\beta x} \D x)$ and $V = H_{0}^{1}(0,\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}, \E^{\beta x} \D x)$ for some $\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}>0$ and $\beta > 0$. The Laplacian $\Delta \in \mathcal{L}(V,V^{*})$ is defined via the duality product as follows:  
\begin{align}\label{eq:Laplacian}
    (\Delta f)(g) \coloneqq \langle f', g' \rangle_{V}, \quad f, g \in V,
\end{align}
with zero Dirichlet boundary conditions. It is well known that the Laplacian has
an orthonormal family of eigenvectors $(e_{n})_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ in $H$, given
by   
\begin{align*}
    e_{n}(x) = \sin\left( \frac{n\pi}{\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}} x \right), \quad n
  \in \mathbb{N}, 
\end{align*}
with an associated sequence of eigenvalues $(\lambda_{n})_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$
given by 
\begin{align*}
    \lambda_{n} = -\frac{1}{\beta} \left( \frac{n\pi}{\Theta_{\mathrm{max}}}
  \right)^{2}, \quad n \in \mathbb{N}. 
\end{align*}
Moreover, it holds that
\begin{align*}
  \alpha_{0} \norm{f}^{2}_{V}\leq \langle \Delta f, f \rangle_{H} \leq
  \alpha_{1} \norm{f}^{2}_{V}
\end{align*}
for some $\alpha_{0},\alpha_{1}>0$ and for all $f \in V$, thus satisfying part (c)
of~\cref{item:linear-operator-unbounded}. The restriction of $-\Delta$ to
\begin{align*}
    \dom(A) \coloneqq \set{f \in V \colon \Delta f \in H}
\end{align*}
is a negative self-adjoint operator that generates the \emph{heat semigroup}
$(T(t))_{t \geq 0}$ on the space $V$. This semigroup gives its name to the
\emph{heat-modulated stochastic volatility model} presented in this work. The
heat semigroup $(T(t))_{t \geq 0}$ is a uniformly stable and analytic semigroup
of bounded, self-adjoint operators on $V$.
\end{example}

Let $(e_{i})_{i\in\MN}$ be the sequence of eigenvectors of $B$ conform part
  (a) of~\cref{item:linear-operator-unbounded}. Let $i\leq j$, then
  $e_{i}$ and $e_{j}$ are two eigenvectors of $B$ with eigenvalue $-\lambda_{i}$
  and $-\lambda_{j}$, respectively. For every $i\in\MN$, we then define $\be_{i,i}\df e_{i}\otimes e_{i}$, and for all distinct $i$ and $j$, we
set $\be_{i,j}\df \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(e_{i}\otimes e_{j}+e_{j}\otimes
e_{i})$. Note that $\norm{\be_{i,j}}=1$, $\be_{i,j}=\be_{j,i}$ for every
$i,j\in\MN$ and it can be seen that the family $\set{\be_{i,j}}_{i\leq
  j\in\MN}\df\set{\be_{i,j}\colon i,j\in\MN, i\leq j}$ is an orthonormal basis
of $\cH$ and that for $h\in H$ we have 
    \begin{align*}
      \be_{i,j}B^{*}(h)=e_{i}\otimes e_{j}B^{*}(h)=e_{j}\langle
      e_{i},B^{*}h\rangle=e_{j}\langle B e_{i},h\rangle=-\lambda_{i}
      e_{i}\otimes e_{j}. 
    \end{align*}
    Hence, by defining the \emph{Lyapunov operator} of $B$, denoted $L\colon
    \dom(L)\subseteq\cH\to \cH$, as
    \begin{align}\label{eq:Lyapunov}
      L(x)\df Bx+xB^{*},\quad x\in \dom(L),
    \end{align}
    and setting $\lambda_{i,j}\df \lambda_{i}+\lambda_{j}$, we obtain
    \begin{align}\label{eq:Lyapunov-eigenvalues}
      L(\be_{i,j}) &=L(e_{i}\otimes e_{j})+L(e_{j}\otimes e_{i})\nonumber\\
                   &=B e_{i}\otimes e_{j}+e_{i}\otimes e_{j}B^{*}+B
                     e_{j}\otimes e_{i}+e_{j}\otimes e_{i}B^{*}\nonumber\\
                   &= -\lambda_{j}(e_{i}\otimes e_{j}+e_{j}\otimes e_{i})-\lambda_{i}(e_{i}\otimes
                     e_{j}+e_{j}\otimes e_{i})\nonumber\\
                   &=-\lambda_{i,j}\be_{i,j}.
    \end{align}
    This shows that for $i\leq j$ the vector $\be_{i,j}$ is an eigenvector of
    the operator $L$ with eigenvalue $-\lambda_{i,j}$. Note that
    $\lambda_{i,j}=\lambda_{j,i}$ and
    $ 0\leq \lambda_{i,j}\leq \lambda_{i+n,j+m}$ for every
    $n,m\in \MN\cup\set{0}$ and $\lambda_{i,j}\to\infty$ for $i\to\infty$ or
    $j\to\infty$. Moreover, we denote by $(\cT(t))_{t\geq 0}$ the strongly
    continuous operator semigroup on $\cH$ generated by $(L,\dom(L))$.

    \begin{remark}
    By part (c) in~\cref{item:linear-operator-unbounded} it follows that the
    operator $L$ 
    satisfies the following coercivity assumption on $\cH$: there exists a
    $\beta_{0},\beta_{1}>0$ and $\eta\geq 0$ such that 
    \begin{align*}
      \beta_{0}\norm{u}^{2}_{\cV}\leq 2 \langle L(u),u\rangle_{\cH}+\eta
      \norm{u}^{2}\leq \beta_{1}\norm{u}_{\cV}^{2}\quad\text{for all } u\in\cV,
    \end{align*}
    where $\langle L(u),u\rangle =L(u)(u)$ denotes the linear map
    $L(u)\in \cV^{*}$ applied to $u\in \cV$.  
    \end{remark}

    Let $(b,\mathbf{B},m,\mu)$ be an admissible parameter set conform
    Definition~\ref{def:admissible-irregular}. Next, define the functions
    $F \colon \cHplus \to \MR$ by 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:F}
      F(u)= \langle b, u \rangle - \int_{\cHpluso} \big( \E^{-\langle \xi, u
      \rangle} - 1 + \langle \chi(\xi), u \rangle \big) \, \dm, 
    \end{align}
    and $\hat{R} \colon \cHplus \to \cH$ by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:R-hat}
      \hat{R}(u)= \Gamma^{*}(u) - \int_{\cHpluso} \big( \E^{-\langle \xi, u \rangle}
      - 1 + \langle \chi(\xi), u \rangle \big) \frac{\dmu}{\norm{\xi}^{2}}. 
    \end{align}
    The functions $F$ and $\hat{R}$ are well-defined and locally Lipschitz
    continuous on $\cHplus$, see~\cite[Remark 3.4]{CKK22a}. The functions $F$
    and $\hat{R}$, together with the Lyapunov operator $L$
    from~\eqref{eq:Lyapunov} and an intial value $(0,u)$ with $u \in \cHplus$
    give rise to the following pair of differential equations, which we refer to
    as the \emph{generalized mild Riccati equations}:  
\begin{subequations}
  \begin{empheq}[left=\empheqlbrace]{align}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial t}\phi(t,u) &=
    F(\psi(t,u)), \label{eq:Riccati-phi-mild} &\phi(0,u)=0,\\ 
    \frac{\partial }{\partial t}\psi(t,u) &= L(\psi(t,u)) +
    \hat{R}(\psi(t,u)), &\psi(0,u)=u. \label{eq:Riccati-psi-mild}
  \end{empheq}
\end{subequations}

To solve the generalized mild Riccati equations
\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-mild}–\eqref{eq:Riccati-psi-mild}, we introduce a spectral
Galerkin-type approximation. For every $d \in \mathbb{N}$, let $H_d$ denote the
$d$-dimensional subspace of $H$ spanned by the first $d$ eigenvectors, i.e.,
$$H_d \coloneqq \operatorname{span}\{e_i \colon i = 1, \dots, d\},$$ and we define the
orthogonal projection of $H$ onto $H_d$, with respect to the inner product
$\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_H$, by $\sP_d$.

For each $d \in \mathbb{N}$, let $\cH_d$ represent the finite-dimensional subspace of $\cH$ spanned by the family $\{\be_{i,j} \colon 1 \leq i \leq j \leq d\}$, i.e.,
\begin{align*}
\cH_d \coloneqq \operatorname{span}\{\be_{i,j} \colon 1 \leq i \leq j \leq d\},  
\end{align*}
and let $\bP_d$ denote the orthogonal projection of $\cH$ onto $\cH_d$, with
respect to the inner product $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle$. For every
$d \in \mathbb{N}$ and $u \in \cH$, we have $\bP_d(u) = \sP_d u
\sP_d$. Moreover, every operator in $\cH_d$ is self-adjoint and of rank at most
$d$. We define $\bP_d^\perp(u) \coloneqq u - \bP_d(u)$ and note that
$\lim_{d \to \infty} \|\bP_d^\perp x\| = 0$ for all $x \in \cH$.

Additionally, we observe that $\cH_d = \{u \sP_d \colon u \in \cL(H_d), u = u^*\}$, and for the cone of all positive self-adjoint operators in $\cH_d$, denoted by $\cH^+_d$, we have:
\begin{align*}
\cH^+_d \coloneqq \{u \sP_d \colon u \in \cL(H_d), u = u^*, \, (u h, h)_H \geq 0 \,\, \forall h \in H_d\}.  
\end{align*}

Importantly, for all $d \in \mathbb{N}$, we have the nested inclusion
$\cH^+_d \subseteq \cH^+_{d+1} \subseteq \cHplus$. For more details on the
subspace of finite-rank operators in the space of all Hilbert-Schmidt operators,
see \cite{Ros91}. Following \cite{Goe84}, we refer to a sequence
$(\cH_d, \bP_d)_{d \in \mathbb{N}}$, as defined above, as a \emph{projection
  scheme} in $\cH$ (with respect to the basis $\{\be_{i,j}\}_{i \leq j \in
  \mathbb{N}}$). 

\begin{definition}
We define the
$d^{{\tiny th}}$-Galerkin approximation of~\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-mild}-\eqref{eq:Riccati-psi-mild} as the unique solutions
$\phi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u))\in C^{1}(\MRplus,\MRplus)$ and
$\psi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u))\in C^{1}(\MRplus,\cHplus_{d})$ to 
  \begin{subequations}
    \begin{empheq}[left=\empheqlbrace]{align}
      \,\frac{\partial}{\partial
        t}\phi_{d}(t,\bP_{d}(u))&=F_{d}(\psi_{d}(t,\bP_{d}(u))), &\phi_{d}(0,\bP_{d}(u))=0, \label{eq:mild-Riccati-Galerkin-phi} \\
      \,\frac{\partial}{\partial
        t}\psi_{d}(t,\bP_{d}(u))&=R_{d}(\psi_{d}(t,\bP(u))), &\psi_{d}(0,\bP_{d}(u))=\bP_{d}(u),\label{eq:mild-Riccati-Galerkin-psi}
    \end{empheq} 
  \end{subequations}
  where $F_{d}\colon\cHplus_{d}\to \MR$ is defined by $F_{d}(u_{d})\df
  F(u_{d})$ and $R_{d}\colon\cHplus_{d}\to \cH_{d}$ is defined for every $u_{d}\in
  \cHplus_{d}$ as 
\begin{align}\label{eq:R-Galerkin-irregular}
  R_{d}(u_{d})= L(u_{d})+\hat{R}_{d}(u_{d})=Bu_{d}+u_{d}B^{*}+\hat{R}_{d}(u_{d}),
\end{align}
with $\hat{R}_{d}(u_{d})\df\bP_{d}(\hat{R}(u_{d}))$ for
$u_{d}\in \cHplus_{d}$. 
\end{definition}


\begin{proposition}\label{prop:existence-mild-Riccati}
Let $(b,\mathbf{B},m,\mu)$ be an admissible parameter as defined in
Definition~\ref{def:admissible-irregular}. Then, for every $d\in\MN$, $T>0$
and $u\in\cHplus$, there exists a unique solution
$\big(\phi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u)),\psi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u))\big)$ to the system
of equations~\eqref{eq:mild-Riccati-Galerkin-phi}-\eqref{eq:mild-Riccati-Galerkin-psi}
such that $\phi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u))\in C^{1}([0,T],\MRplus)$ and
$\psi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u))\in C^{1}([0,T],\cHplus_{d})$. Moreover,
the Galerkin approximations $\phi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u))$ and $\psi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u)))$ converge
uniformly on $[0,T]$ to $\phi(\cdot,u)\in C^{1}([0,T],\MRplus)$
and $\psi(\cdot,u)\in C([0,T],\cHplus)$, the unique solutions
to the system~\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-mild}-\eqref{eq:Riccati-psi-mild}, respectively. The
solution $\psi(\cdot,u)$ satisfies the variation-of-constant formula:
\begin{align}\label{eq:mild-solution-variation}
  \psi(t,u)=\cT(t)u + \int_{0}^{t} \cT(t-s)R(\psi(s,u))\D s,\quad t\in [0,T].  
\end{align}
Furthermore, there exists a constant $K>0$ such that
\begin{align}\label{eq:convergence-mild-Galerkin}
\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\big(|\phi_{d}(t,\bP_{d}(u))-\phi(t,u)|+\norm{\psi_{d}(t,\bP_{d}(u))-\psi(t,u)}\big)\leq
  K C_{T,d},  
\end{align}
where
$C_{T,d}=\norm{\bP_{d}^{\perp}(u)}+\norm{\bP_{d}^{\perp}(\mu(\cHpluso))}$. 
\end{proposition}

The well-posedness of the generalized (mild) Riccati equations is closely
related to the existence of an associated class of \emph{affine processes},
which we first define for the Galerkin approximations and subsequently show that
the affine processes associated with the approximations converge to the desired
irregular affine process.

For each $d \in \mathbb{N}$, $F_{d}(u_{d})\df
  F(u_{d})$ and $R_d$ defined as in
\eqref{eq:R-Galerkin-irregular}, we define the set
$\cD^d \coloneqq \{\E^{-\langle \cdot, u \rangle} \colon u \in \cHplus_d\}
\subseteq C(\cHplus, \mathbb{R})$, and the operator
$\cG^d \colon \cD^d \to C(\cHplus, \mathbb{R})$ as:
\begin{align}\label{eq:G-d-operator}
  \cG^d \E^{-\langle \,\cdot\,, u \rangle}(x) \coloneqq \left(-F_d(u) - \langle x, R_d(u) \rangle\right) \E^{-\langle x, u \rangle}, \quad x \in \cHplus.
\end{align}

The following proposition asserts that for all $d \in \mathbb{N}$, the
$d^{\text{th}}$-Galerkin approximation
$\big(\phi_d(\cdot, \bP_d(u)), \psi_d(\cdot, \bP_d(u))\big)$ gives rise to an
affine Markov process $X^d$ with values in $\cHplus_d$, which solves the
martingale problem for $\cG^d$ with $X^d_0 = \bP_d(x)$ on a suitable stochastic
basis. This is the \emph{irregular} version of~\cite[Proposition
3.3]{karbach2023finiterank}.


\begin{proposition}\label{prop:embedding-affine-main}
  Assume that the conditions of Proposition~\ref{prop:existence-mild-Riccati}
  hold. Then for every $d\in\MN$, the following holds:
  \begin{propenum}
  \item[i)]\label{item:embedding-affine-main-1} There exists a unique Markov
    process $(X^{d},(\MP_{x}^{d})_{x\in\cHplus})$, realized on the space
    $D(\MRplus,\cHplus)$ of all c\`adl\`ag paths and where $\MP_{x}^{d}$
    denotes the law of $X^{d}$ given $X_{0}^{d}=\bP_{d}(x)$, such that for all
    $x\in\cHplus$ we have $\MP_{x}^{d}(\set{X^{d}_{t}\in\cHplus_{d}\colon\,
      t\geq 0})=1$ and the following affine transform formula holds true:    
    \begin{align}\label{eq:affine-Galerkin}
      \hspace{10mm} \EXspec{\mathbb{P}^{d}_{x}}{\E^{-\langle X^{d}_{t},
      \bP_{d}(u)\rangle}}=\E^{-\phi_{d}(t,\bP_{d}(u))-\langle
      \bP_{d}(x),\psi_{d}(t,\bP_{d}(u))\rangle},\,\, t\geq 0,\,u\in\cHplus,  
    \end{align}
    for $\big(\phi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u)),\psi_{d}(\cdot,\bP_{d}(u))\big)$ the
    unique solution
    of~\eqref{eq:mild-Riccati-Galerkin-phi}-\eqref{eq:mild-Riccati-Galerkin-psi}. 
  \item[ii)]\label{item:embedding-affine-main-2} For every $x\in\cHplus$
    and every $u\in\cHplus$ the process 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:Gd-martingale-problem}
      \hspace{10mm}\Big(\E^{-\langle X_{t}^{d},\bP_{d}(u)\rangle}-\E^{-\langle
      \bP_{d}(x),\bP_{d}(u)\rangle}-\int_{0}^{t}(\cG^{d}\E^{-\langle
      \,\cdot\,,\bP_{d}(u)\rangle})(X_{s}^{d})\,\D s\Big)_{t\geq 0},
    \end{align}
    is a real-valued martingale with respect to the stochastic basis
    $(\Omega,\bar{\cF}^{d},\bar{\MF}^{d},\!\MP_{x}^{d})$, where
    $\Omega=D(\MRplus,\cHplus)$ and $\bar{\MF}^{d}=(\bar{\cF}^{d}_{t})_{t\geq
      0}$ denotes the augmentation of
    the natural filtration $(\cF^{d}_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ of $X^{d}$ with respect to
    the measure $\MP_{x}^{d}$ from i).
  \end{propenum}
\end{proposition}


Next, we show that any \textit{admissible parameter set}
$(b, \mathbf{B}, m, \mu)$ give rise to an affine process with values in
$\cHplus\cap \cL_{1}(H)\cap \cV$ and that the sequence of finite-rank
operator-valued processes $(X^{d})_{d\in\MN}$ converges weakly to this limiting process.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main-convergence}
  Let $(b,\mathbf{B},m,\mu)$ be an admissible parameter set as defined in
  Definition~\ref{def:admissible-irregular}. Then, the following holds: 
  \begin{theoremenum}
  \item\label{item:main-convergence-1} There exists a unique affine process
    $(X,(\MP_{x})_{x\in\cHplus})$ on $\cHplus$, with paths in
    $D(\MRplus,\cHplus)$ and where $\MP_{x}$ denotes the law of $X$ given
    $X_{0}=x$, such that for every $x\in\cHplus$ we have  
    \begin{align}\label{eq:affine-formula-main}
      \EXspec{\MP_{x}}{\E^{-\langle X_{t}, u\rangle}}=\E^{-\phi(t,u)-\langle
      x,\psi(t,u)\rangle},\quad t\geq 0,\, u\in\cHplus,  
    \end{align}
    for $(\phi(\cdot,u),\psi(\cdot,u))$ the unique mild solution
    of~\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-mild}-\eqref{eq:Riccati-psi-mild}. Furthermore, it
    holds that $X_{t}\in \cV\cap\cL_{1}(H)$ for every $t\geq 0$.
  \item\label{item:main-convergence-2} Let $(X^{d})_{d\in\MN}$
    be the sequence of finite-rank operator-valued affine processes in
    Proposition~\ref{prop:embedding-affine-main}. Then
    the sequence $(X^{d})_{d\in\MN}$ converges weakly to $X$ on $D(\MRplus,\cHplus)$
    equipped with the Skorohod topology, i.e. it holds
    \begin{align*}
      \EXspec{\MP_{x}^{d}}{f(X^{d})}\to \EXspec{\MP_{x}}{f(X)},\quad\text{as
      }d\to\infty, 
    \end{align*}
    for all $f\in C(D(\MRplus,\cHplus),\MR)$.
  \end{theoremenum}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
  Note that the affine processes $(X, (\MP_x)_{x \in \cHplus})$ on $\cHplus$ from
  Theorem~\ref{thm:main-convergence} are irregular, in the sense that the derivative
  $\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\psi(t,u)\lvert_{t=0}$, cf.~\cite[Definition 2.2]{CFMT11}, is not continuous at
  $u=0$, due to the unboundedness of the Lyapunov operator $L$ on $\cH$. However, the
  coercivity assumption ensures that this irregular affine process takes values
  in the space of trace-class operators and in $\cV$. Specifically, this implies
  that $X_t \in \cL_2(H, V)$, qualifying $(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ as a valid
  instantaneous volatility process according to
  Assumption~\ref{assump:integration}.  Furthermore, if in part (a) of
  \cref{item:linear-operator-unbounded} the stronger condition $B\in\cL(H)$ is
  satisfied, then the admissible parameter set is also admissible in
  the regular sense of \cite[Definition 2.3]{CKK22a} and the existence in part
  i) of Theorem~\ref{thm:main-convergence} would follow from~\cite{CKK22a}, but
  $X_{t}$ is not trace class.
\end{remark}


\section{Finite-Rank Approximations of Heat-Modulated Affine SV
  Models}\label{sec:the-affine-model}

In this section, we examine the joint stochastic covariance model
$(f_{t}, \sigma_{t}^{2})_{t \geq 0}$, where $(f_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ represents the
mild solution to the HJMM equation~\eqref{eq:HJMM-SDE} modulated by a stochastic
volatility process $(\sigma_{t})_{t \geq 0}$. We define
$(\sigma_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ as the square-root process of an irregular affine
process $(X_{t})_{t \geq 0}$ on $\cHplus$, associated with the admissible
parameter set $(b, \mathbf{B}, m, \mu)$, the existence of which is guaranteed by
Theorem~\ref{thm:main-convergence}. The main result of this section is that the
joint model $(f_{t}, \sigma_{t}^{2})_{t \geq 0}$ satisfies an affine transform
formula (see Theorem~\ref{thm:heat-affine-model} below) and can be approximated
by finite-rank affine stochastic covariance models via a spectral Galerkin
approximation of the generalized mild Riccati equations (see
Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-dim-approx-ScoV} below). We conclude this section with
an example of heat-modulated affine stochastic volatility models, as well as
an application of the theory to the pricing of options written on \emph{power
  flow forwards}.

 \subsection{The Heat-Modulated Affine Stochastic Covariance
   Model}\label{sec:abstr-affine-stoch}

 As before, we consider the Hilbert space triple
 $V\hookrightarrow H \hookrightarrow V^{*}$ compact, with $V$ the state space of regular
 forward curves. This triple of forward curve spaces induces the triple of
 Hilbert-Schmidt operators given by
 $\cV\hookrightarrow \cH \hookrightarrow \cV^{*}$, with
 $\cV=\cL_{2}(V^{*},H)\cap\cL_{2}(H,V)$.

 Let $(b,\mathbf{B},m,\mu)$ be an admissible parameter set as in
 Definition~\ref{def:admissible-irregular}, and denote by $(X_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ the 
 associated irregular affine process on $\cHplus$ given by
 Theorem~\ref{thm:main-convergence}. Moreover, denote the filtered
 probability space of $X$ by
 $(\Omega^{(1)},\cF^{(2)},(\mathcal{F}_t^{(2)})_{t\geq 0}, \mathbb{P}^{(1)})$
 and fix an initial value $\mathbb{P}^{(1)}(X_0=x)=1$ for $x\in\cHplus$. We let
 $(\Omega^{(2)},\cF^{(2)}, (\mathcal{F}_t^{(2)})_{t\geq 0}, \mathbb{P}^{(2)})$
 be another filtered probability space satisfying the usual conditions and that
 carries a cylindrical Brownian motion $W\colon [0,\infty)\times H \rightarrow
 L^{2}(\Omega^{(2)},\cF^{(2)},\mathbb{P}^{(2)})$, in the following denoted by $(W_{t})_{t\geq
   0}$. We assume that
 $\mathbb{P}^{(2)}(f_{0}=F(0,\cdot))=1$ for some initial forward curve
 $F(0,\cdot)\in H$ and define the following joint filtered probability space:
\begin{align*}
(\Omega, \cF, \MF, \MP)\df(\Omega^{(1)}\times\Omega^{(2)}, (\cF^{(1)}\otimes \cF^{(2)}),
(\cF^{(1)}_{t}\otimes \cF_{t}^{(2)})_{t\geq 0}, \MP^{(1)}\otimes \MP^{(2)}).
\end{align*}
We denote the expectation with respect to $\MP$ by $\mathbb{E}$ and from now on
consider $(X_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ and the cylindrical Brownian motion $(W_{t})_{t\geq
0}$ to be defined on $(\Omega,\cF,\MF,\MP)$.\par

\subsubsection{The Heat Modulated Affine Stochastic Volatility Model is Affine}

We define a new set of generalized mild Riccati equation associated to the joint
process $(f_{t},X_{t})_{t\geq 0}$. For this, define $\hat{\cR}\colon \I H \times
\cHplus \to \cH$ as  
\begin{align}
 \hat{\cR}(h,u)&= \Gamma^{*}(u)-\tfrac{1}{2}h\otimes
    h-\int_{\cHpluso}\big(\E^{-\langle
    \xi,u\rangle}-1+\langle \chi(\xi), u\rangle\big)\frac{\mu(\D \xi)}{\norm{\xi}^{2}},  \label{eq:R-intro}
\end{align}
where for two elements $x$ and $y$ in $H$ we define the operator
$x\otimes y\in \cL(H)$ by $x\otimes y(h)=\langle x,h \rangle_{H} y$ for every
$h\in H$ and write $x^{\otimes 2}\df x\otimes x$.\par{}

Let $T>0$, $u_{1}\in \I H$ and $u_{2}\in\cHplus$. We define the new system of
\textit{generalized joint mild Riccati equations} as follows:  
\begin{subequations}
\begin{empheq}[left=\empheqlbrace]{align}
   \,\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Phi(t,u)&=F(\psi_{2}(t,u)),  &\quad\Phi(0,u)=0,\label{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-1}\\
    \,\psi_{1}(t,u)&=u_{1}-\I \cA^{*}\left(\I\int_{0}^{t}\psi_{1}(s,u)\D
                   s\right),  &\quad\psi_{1}(0,u)=u_{1}, \label{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-2}\\
    \,\frac{\partial }{\partial t}\psi_{2}(t,u)&=L(\psi_{2}(t,u))+\hat{\cR}(\psi_{1}(t,u),
    \psi_{2}(t,u)),  & \quad
                                                  \psi_{2}(0,u)=u_{2}.\label{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-3} 
       \end{empheq}
\end{subequations}


For every initial value $u=(u_{1},u_{2})\in \I H\times \cHplus$, we say that
$(\Phi(\cdot, u),\Psi(\cdot, u))$ defined as
$(\Phi(\cdot, u),\Psi(\cdot, u))\df(\Phi(\cdot, u),(\psi_{1}(\cdot,
u),\psi_{2}(\cdot, u)))\colon [0,T] \to \R \times \I H\times \cH$ is a
\emph{mild solution}
to~\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-1}-\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-3} if
$\Phi(\cdot,u)\in C^{1}([0,T];\MRplus)$, $\psi_{1}(\cdot,u)\in C([0,T];\I H)$,
$\psi_{2}(\cdot,u)\in C([0,T];\cHplus)$ and the map
$(\Phi(\cdot,u),\Psi(\cdot,u))$ satisfies
\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-1}-\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-3}.


\begin{theorem}\label{thm:heat-affine-model}
  Let $(f, X)$ be the stochastic covariance model where $(f_t)_{t \geq 0}$ is
  given by \eqref{eq:HJMM-SDE}, modulated by the instantaneous covariance process
  $(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$, which is the affine process associated with $(b,
  \mathbf{B}, m, \mu)$ as defined in
  Theorem~\ref{thm:main-convergence}. Furthermore, let $u = (u_1, u_2) \in \I H
  \times \cH$ and $(\Phi(\cdot, u), (\psi_1(\cdot, u), \psi_2(\cdot, u)))$ be
  the solution to the generalized joint mild Riccati equations
  \eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-1}–\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-3}. Then, for all
  $t \geq 0$, the following holds: 
  \begin{align}
    \label{eq:extended-affine-formula}
    \mathbb{E} \left[\E^{\langle f_t, u_1 \rangle_H - \langle X_{t}, u_2
    \rangle} \right] = \E^{-\Phi(t, u) + \langle f_0, \psi_1(t, u) \rangle_H -
    \langle x, \psi_2(t, u) \rangle}. 
  \end{align}
  If moreover, $f_{0}\in V$ and $X^{1/2}_{t}\in\cV$ for all $t\geq 0$, then
  $(f_{t},X_{t})\in V\times \cV$ for all $t\geq 0$ and
  Assumption~\ref{assump:integration} is satisfied for $\sigma_{t}=X_{t}^{1/2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[The heat-modulated BNS stochastic
  volatility model]  

  As an example of a heat-modulated affine stochastic volatility model, we
  introduce the \emph{heat-modulated Barndorff–Nielsen and Shepard (BNS)
    stochastic volatility model}. This is a heat-modulated version of the
  Hilbert space-valued BNS model presented in~\cite{BRS18, BS24}. In the
  heat-modulated BNS stochastic volatility model we assume that the forward
  curve process $(f_t)_{t \geq 0}$ is given by the HJMM equation
  \eqref{eq:HJMM-SDE} on the state space 
  $H = L^2(0, \Theta_{\mathrm{max}}, \E^{\beta x}\D x)$, for some
  $\Theta_{\mathrm{max}} > 0$ and $\beta > 0$, and is modulated by an
  instantaneous volatility process $(\sigma_t)_{t \geq 0}$, which is the
  operator square root of an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process $(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ on
  $\cHplus$, given by:
  \begin{align} 
    \begin{cases}
      \D X_t &= L_{\Delta}(X_t) \D t + \D L_t,\\
      X_0 &= x \in \cHplus, 
    \end{cases}
  \end{align}

  where $(L_t)_{t \geq 0}$ is a $\cH$-valued L\'evy process with almost surely
  increasing paths, and $L_{\Delta}$ denotes the Lyapunov operator of the Laplacian
  as described in Example~\ref{ex:Laplacian}. We denote by $\varphi_{L}$ for the
  Laplace exponent of the L\'evy process $L$ with characteristics $(b,0,m)$, i.e. 
  \begin{align}\label{eq:Laplace-Levysubordinator}
    \varphi_{L}(u)= \langle b, u\rangle-\int_{\cHpluso}\big(\E^{-\langle \xi,
    u\rangle}-1+\one_{\norm{\xi}\leq 1}(\xi)\langle \xi, u\rangle\big)\,m(\D
    \xi)\, ,\quad u\in \cHplus,   
  \end{align}
  with drift $b\in\cHplus$ and L\'evy measure $m(\D\xi)$ defined on $\cB(\cHpluso)$,
  see~\cite{Det77}.

The semigroup $\cT(t) = \E^{tL_{\Delta}}$ satisfies $\cT(t)u =
\E^{t\Delta}u\E^{t\Delta}$ for $t \geq 0$, and maps $\cHplus$ into $\cHplus$ for
all $t \geq 0$. Moreover, the process $(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ is indeed a stochastically
continuous affine Markov process with value in $\cHplus$, associated with the
admissible parameter set $(b, L_{\Delta}, m, 0)$. This can be seen by solving
the generalized joint mild Riccati equations
\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-1}–\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-3} explicitly and
inserting the solutions into the affine transform formula
\eqref{eq:extended-affine-formula}.

When computing the characteristic function of the process $(f_t, X_t)_{t \geq
    0}$, it coincides with the affine transform formula, as described in
  \cite[Section 4.1]{CKK22b}. Specifically, in the heat-modulated BNS stochastic
  volatility model, the characteristic function of the forward curve process
  $(f_t)_{t \geq 0}$ is given by:
  \begin{align}\label{eq:affine-transform-levy}
    \mathbb{E}\left[\exp\left(\I \langle f_t, u_1 \rangle_H\right)\right] &= \exp\left(\I \langle f_0, S^*(t) u_1 \rangle_H\right) \nonumber \\
    &\quad\times \exp\left(-\int_0^t \varphi_L\left(\tfrac{1}{2} \int_0^s \cT(s - \tau) (S^*(\tau) u_1)^{\otimes 2} \D \tau\right) \D s\right) \nonumber \\
    &\quad\times \exp\left(-\tfrac{1}{2} \langle x, \int_0^t \cT(\tau) (S^*(t -
      \tau) u_1)^{\otimes 2} \D \tau \rangle\right). 
  \end{align}
\end{example}

The following theorem is our main result on the approximation of affine
function-valued stochastic covariance models by affine finite-rank models. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:finite-dim-approx-ScoV}
  Let $(f,X)$ denote the stochastic covariance model from
  Theorem~\ref{thm:heat-affine-model} with $(f_{0},X_{0})=(F(\cdot,0),x)\in
  H\times\cHplus$. For every $d\in\MN$ let $X^{d}$ denote
  the affine process on $\cHplus_{d}$ as in
  Proposition~\ref{prop:embedding-affine-main} and define $(f^{d},X^{d})$ as the
  stochastic covariance model with $(f^{d}_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ given
  by~\eqref{eq:HJMM-SDE}, modulated by the finite-rank instantaneous volatility
  process $(X_{t}^{d})^{1/2}$. Then the following holds: 
  \begin{theoremenum}
  \item\label{item:finite-dim-approx-ScoV-1} For every $f_{0}\in H$,
    $x\in\cHplus$ the stochastic covariance model $(f^{d},X^{d})$ with
    $(f^{d}_{0},X_{0}^{d})=(f_{0},\bP_{d}(x)) $ is well-defined and for
    every $u=(u_{1},u_{2})\in \I H\times\cHplus_{d}$ and $t\geq 0$ satisfies  
    \begin{align}\label{eq:stochastic-covariance-affine-formula}
     \EX{\E^{\langle f^{d}_{t}, u_{1}\rangle_{H}- \langle X^{d}_{t},
      u_{2}\rangle}}=\E^{-\Phi_{d}(t,u)+\langle f_{0}, 
      \psi_{1}(t,u)\rangle_{H}-\langle \bP_{d}(x),
      \psi_{2,d}(t,u)\rangle}, 
    \end{align}
    for $(\Phi_{d}(\cdot,u),(\psi_{1}(\cdot,u),\psi_{2,d}(\cdot,u)))$ the
    unique solution
    of~\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-1}-~\eqref{eq:Riccati-phi-psi-1-3} with $F$
    and $L(\cdot)+\hat{\cR}$ replaced by $F\circ\bP_{d}$ and $\bP_{d}\circ L
    \circ \bP_{d}+\bP_{d}\circ \hat{\cR} \circ \bP_{d}$, respectively. 
  \item\label{item:finite-dim-approx-ScoV-2} The sequence
    $(f^{d},X^{d})_{d\in\MN}$ converges weakly to $(f,X)$ as the rank
    $d\in\MN$ tends to infinity, i.e. for all functions $g\in
    C(D(\MRplus,H\times\cHplus),\MR)$  we have 
    \begin{align*}
      \EX{g(f^{d},X^{d})}\to \EX{g(f,X)},\quad\text{as }d\to\infty. 
    \end{align*}
    If, in addition, $\norm{\mu(\cHpluso)}_{\cV}<\infty$ and
    $\Gamma^{*}(\cV\cap \cH)\subseteq\cV\cap \cH$, then for every $T>0$ and
    $\tilde{u}\in H$ such that $\norm{\tilde{u}}_{V}\leq 1$ there exists a constant
    $\tilde{C}_{T}$ such that for all $d\in\MN$ we have
    \begin{align}\label{eq:stochastic-covariance-convergence-rate}
      \sup_{t\in [0,T]}
      \left|\EX{\E^{\I \langle f_{t}, \tilde{u}\rangle_{H}}}-\EX{\E^{\I\langle
      f^{d}_{t},\tilde{u}\rangle_{H}}}\right|\leq
      \tilde{C}_{T}\norm{\bP_{d}^{\perp}}_{\cL(\cV,\cH)}(1+\norm{x}).
    \end{align}
  \end{theoremenum}
\end{theorem}


\begin{example}
  As an application of the approximation result from
  Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-dim-approx-ScoV}, we analyse the robustness of pricing
  a
  European Call option written on a \emph{power flow forward} in energy markets,
  as described in \cite{BK15}. A power flow forward is a standard contract in
  electricity and gas markets, based on the delivery flow of electricity or gas
  over a pre-specified period $[\tau_1, \tau_2]$ for $\tau_1 < \tau_2$, and
  is modeled as the average price of the \emph{instantaneous forward price}
  $F(t,T)$ over $T \in [\tau_1, \tau_2]$:
  \begin{align*}
    F(t, \tau_1, \tau_2) = \frac{1}{\tau_2 - \tau_1} \int_{\tau_1}^{\tau_2} F(t,T) \, \D T,
  \end{align*}
  and using the time-to-maturity notation $F(t, \tau_1, \tau_2) =
  \frac{1}{\tau_2 - \tau_1} \int_{\tau_1-t}^{\tau_2-t} f(t,z) \, \D z$. 

  A European Call option with strike $K$ and exercise date $t < \tau_1$ has
  payoff given by 
  \begin{align*}
    H_t = \left(F(t, \tau_1, \tau_2) - K\right)^+.
  \end{align*}

  Using Fourier inversion techniques as in \cite[Proposition 4.7]{Kar22}, a semi-closed
  formula can be derived for pricing European contingent claims written on the
  forward curve in affine stochastic covariance models. In the following
  corollary, we first provide the fair price of a European Call option at time 0
  written on a flow forward, and then present a robustness result for option
  prices regarding finite-rank approximations using the convergence rates
  deduced in Proposition~\ref{prop:existence-mild-Riccati} and standard estimates.


\begin{corollary}\label{cor:robustness}
    Let $(f, X)$ be an affine stochastic covariance model on $H$ with a finite-rank approximation $(f^d, X^d)_{t \geq 0}$. Then the following holds:
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item[i)] The price $\pi_0$ of a European Call option at time 0 with exercise time $t$ and strike $K$, written on the flow forward $F(t, \tau_1, \tau_2)$, is given by  
    \begin{align*}
      \pi_0 &= \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{\mathbb{R}} g(\lambda) \E^{-\Phi(t, \Lambda_{\tau_1}^{\tau_2}) + \langle F(0, \cdot), \psi_1(t, \Lambda_{\tau_1}^{\tau_2}) \rangle_H - \langle x, \psi_2(t, \Lambda_{\tau_1}^{\tau_2}) \rangle} \, \D \lambda,
    \end{align*}
    where $g(\lambda) = \frac{K^{-(\eta - 1 + \I \lambda)}}{(\eta + \I
      \lambda)(\eta - 1 + \I \lambda)}$, $\Lambda_{\tau_1}^{\tau_2} =
    \left(\frac{\eta + \I \lambda}{\tau_2 - \tau_1} \int_{\tau_1 - t}^{\tau_2 -
        t} \delta_z(\cdot) \, \D z, 0\right)$ and $\eta \in \mathbb{R}$.

    \item[ii)] There exists a constant $K(t) > 0$ such that for every $d \in
      \mathbb{N}$, we have   
    \begin{align*}
      |\pi_0 - \pi_0^d| \leq K(t) \|\mathbf{P}_d -
      \mathbf{I}\|_{\cL(\mathcal{V}, \cH)} \left(1 + \|\sigma_0^2\|\right) \pi_0
      + \varepsilon_d, 
    \end{align*}  
    where
    \begin{align*}
      F_d(t, \tau_1, \tau_2) = \frac{1}{\tau_2 - \tau_1} \int_{\tau_1 - t}^{\tau_2 - t} f_d(t, z) \, \D z,  
    \end{align*}
    $\pi_0^d = \mathbb{E}\left[\left(F_d(t, \tau_1, \tau_2) -
        K\right)^+\right]$, and $\|\mathbf{P}_d - \mathbf{I}\|_{\cL(\mathcal{V},
      \cH)} \to 0$ and $\varepsilon_d \to 0$ as $d \to \infty$.  
    \end{enumerate}
  \end{corollary}  
\end{example}


\begin{remark}
  We make the followin remarks on approximating the foward curve
  process $(f_{t})_{t\geq 0}$ by finite-dimensional models, and the modeling in $V$ versus $H$:
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item[i)] Note that since the embedding
    $H_{\beta} \subset\!\subset L^2(\mathbb{R}_+, \E^{\gamma x} \D x) \oplus
    \mathbb{R}$ is compact if $0<\gamma<\beta$, we can approximate the forward curve dynamics
    $(f_t)_{t \geq 0}$ by a finite-dimensional process $(U_d(f_t))_{t \geq 0}$
    in the space $L^2(\mathbb{R}_+, \E^{\gamma x} \D x) \oplus \mathbb{R}$,
    where $(U_d)_{d \in \mathbb{N}}$ is defined as the finite-rank operator
    \begin{align*}
    U_d v = \sum_{k=1}^{d} s_k \langle v, h_k^{(d)} \rangle_V e_k  
    \end{align*}
    for some $(s_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}} \subseteq \mathbb{R}_+$ and
    $(h_k^{(d)})_{k \leq d} \subseteq \operatorname{dom}(\mathcal{A}^*)$ (see
    \cite{Tap13}). However, the approximations $(f^d_t)_{t \geq 0}$ are not
    necessarily affine processes, as projections of affine processes are
    generally not affine. Thus, although $(U_d(f_t))_{t \geq 0}$ is
    finite-dimensional, it may not be tractable. Instead, using
    Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-dim-approx-ScoV}, we propose approximating the
    forward curve dynamics by finite-rank affine stochastic covariance models
    $(f^d, X^d)_{d \in \mathbb{N}}$, which, for each $d \in \mathbb{N}$, are
    infinite-dimensional, yet affine and exhibit only finite-rank noise.

  \item[ii)] If there exists an orthonormal basis (ONB)
    $(e_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of eigenvectors for the drift operator
    $\mathcal{A}$ in~\eqref{eq:HJMM-SDE}, we could also approximate the process
    $f^d$ by $H_d$-valued processes $(\tilde{f}^d)_{d \in \mathbb{N}}$ by
    following the same ideas as in this paper, namely approximating
    the unbounded operator $\mathcal{A}$ by $\mathcal{A}^d \coloneqq \sP_d
    \mathcal{A} \sP_d$ and $X$, as presented here, by $X^d$. In this
    case, an analog of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-dim-approx-ScoV} holds, with
    $(f^d, X^d)$ replaced by $(\tilde{f}^d, X^d)$. However, in applications, we
    typically have $\mathcal{A} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}$, which does not
    admit an ONB of eigenvectors in the space $H_{\beta}$. One could, motivated
    by~\cite{Con01}, consider the HJM model with
    $\mathcal{A}_1 \coloneqq \frac{\partial}{\partial x} + \Delta$. This
    operator possesses a sequence of eigenvectors that forms an ONB for spaces
    like $L^2(0, \Theta_{\mathrm{max}}, \E^{\beta x} \D x)$, allowing for
    finite-dimensional and finite-rank approximations. However, such a model may
    not necessarily be arbitrage-free. We leave these considerations for future
    work.

  \item[iii)] If we require modeling the forward curve dynamics in the space of
    regular forward curves $V$ using the heat-modulated affine stochastic
    volatility model, we need to assume $X_{t}^{1/2} \in \cV$ as required by
    Theorem~\ref{thm:heat-affine-model} to satisfy
    Assumption~\ref{assump:integration}. However, note that while
    $X_{t} \in \cV \cap \cL_{1}(H)$ for all $t \geq 0$ (as shown in
    Theorem~\ref{item:main-convergence-1}), this does not necessarily imply
    $X_{t}^{1/2} \in \cV$ for all $t \geq 0$. Indeed, since $X_{t}$ is a
    positive trace-class operator, it can be represented as
    $X_t = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \hat{x}_n(t) \langle x, e_n \rangle e_n$, where
    $(\hat{x}_n(t))_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \in \ell_1$ $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. for all
    $t \geq 0$. However, since $X_t \in \cV$, we also have the representation
    $X_t = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} x_n(t) \langle x, e_n \rangle f_n$, where
    $(x_n(t))_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \in \ell^{2}$ and $(f_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is
    an orthonormal basis (ONB) of $V$. If $V \subset\!\subset H$ compactly, then
    $f_n = \rho_n^{-1} e_n$ for some sequence
    $(\rho_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \subset \mathbb{R}$ such that
    $\rho_n^2 \to \infty$ (see \cite{Ros91}). This implies that
    $(\hat{x}_n(t) \rho_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \in \ell_2$ and
    $(\hat{x}_n(t))_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \in \ell_1$. However, the sequence
    $(\sqrt{x_n(t)} \rho_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ may not belong to $\ell_2$. If
    it does, then we would have $X_{t}^{1/2} \in \cV$ as well.
  \end{enumerate} 
\end{remark}


