\begin{abstract}
Bayes linear estimators are derived by minimizing the Bayes risk with respect to the squared loss function.
Non-unbiased estimators such as an ordinary ridge estimator, a typical shrinkage estimator, and a fractional rank estimator are either Bayes linear estimators or their limit points.
First, we prove that Bayes linear estimators are linearly complete, but not necessarily linearly sufficient.
Second, we derive a necessary and sufficient condition under which two Bayes linear estimators coincide. 
Finally, we obtain an equivalence condition such that the equality between two residual sums of squares holds when Bayes linear estimators are considered.
\\
\\
\textbf{Keywords}: Bayes inference, General linear model, Point estimation\\
\textbf{Mathematics Subject Classification}: 62J05, 62F10, 62F15
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:1}
For positive integers $m_1,m_2$ and $m_3$, let $\mathcal{M}(m_1,m_2)$ and $\mathcal{G}\boldsymbol{\ell}(m_3)$ denote the sets of $m_1 \times m_2$ matrices and $m_3 \times m_3$ nonsingular matrices, respectively. 
For a positive integer $m$, let $\mathcal{S}^+(m)$ and $\mathcal{S}^N(m)$ denote the sets of $m \times m$ symmetric positive definite and symmetric positive semidefinite matrices, respectively. 
Furthermore, let $\boldsymbol{I}_m \in \mathcal{S}^+(m)$ denote the $m\times m$ identity matrix. 
The transpose, Moore--Penrose inverse, rank, trace, column space, and null space of a matrix $\boldsymbol{M}$ are denoted by $\boldsymbol{M}^\top$, $\boldsymbol{M}^+$, $\rk(\boldsymbol{M})$, $\tr(\boldsymbol{M})$, $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{M})$, and $\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{M})$, respectively. 
For matrices $\boldsymbol{P}$ and $\boldsymbol{Q}$ with the same number of rows, $(\boldsymbol{P} \quad \boldsymbol{Q})$ denotes the partitioned matrix with $\boldsymbol{P}$ and $\boldsymbol{Q}$ as submatrices. 
$\| \cdot \|$ stands for the Euclidean norm.
For a subspace $W$ of $\mathbb{R}^n$, $W^\bot$ denotes the orthogonal complement of $W$, that is, $\mathbb{R}^n = W \oplus W^{\bot}$, where $\oplus$ denotes the direct sum.
The expectation vector and covariance matrix of an $m \times 1$ random variable vector $\boldsymbol{u}$ are denoted by $\E[\boldsymbol{u}]$ and $\V(\boldsymbol{u})$, respectively. 
In addition, let $\E_\tau[\cdot]$ denote the expectation with respect to the prior distribution of $\tau$.

Let $n$ and $k$ be positive integers such that $n > k$. 
Throughout this paper, for an observable random vector $\boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n$, we consider the general linear model
\begin{equation*}
 \boldsymbol{y} = \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\beta} +\boldsymbol{\ve}, \quad \E[\boldsymbol{\ve}] = \boldsymbol{0}, \quad \V(\boldsymbol{\ve}) = \sigma^2 \boldsymbol{\Omega},  
\end{equation*}
where  $\boldsymbol{X} \in \mathcal{M}(n,k)$ is a known non-random matrix with $\rk(\boldsymbol{X}) = k$, $\boldsymbol{\beta} \in \mathbb{R}^k$ is an unknown vector, $\boldsymbol{\ve} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is an unobservable random vector, $\sigma^2 \in (0, \infty)$ is an unknown constant, and $\boldsymbol{\Omega} \in \mathcal{S}^+(n)$ is a known matrix. 
A matrix $\boldsymbol{Z} \in \mathcal{M}(n,n-k)$ satisfying $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{Z} = \boldsymbol{0}$ and $\rk(\boldsymbol{Z}) = n-k$ will be fixed throughout the paper. 

For a matrix $\boldsymbol{L} \in \mathcal{M}(n,k)$, let $\boldsymbol{L}^\top\boldsymbol{y}$ be a linear estimator of $\boldsymbol{\beta}$.
Non-unbiased estimators such as an ordinary ridge estimator \citep{RefHK70a, RefHK70b}, a typical shrinkage estimator \citep{RefMW75}, and a Bayes linear estimator \citep{RefR71, RefR76} have been proposed as estimators of $\boldsymbol{\beta}$.
In particular, Bayes linear estimators are preferable because they not only minimize the Bayes risk but also possess linear admissibility and minimaxity.
With a prior distribution for $(\boldsymbol{\beta},\sigma^2)$  such that $\boldsymbol{W} = \E_{\boldsymbol{\beta}}[\boldsymbol{\beta}\boldsymbol{\beta}^\top]$ and $\gamma = \E_{\sigma^2}[\sigma^2]$, Bayes linear estimators are expressed as $\boldsymbol{L}_{*}^\top \boldsymbol{y}$, which satisfies the inequality 
\begin{align}\label{MSE}
\E_{\boldsymbol{\beta},\sigma^2}\left[\E[ \| \boldsymbol{L}_{*}^\top \boldsymbol{y}- \boldsymbol{\beta} \|^2|\ \boldsymbol{\beta},\sigma^2 ]\right] \leq \E_{\boldsymbol{\beta},\sigma^2}\left[\E[ \| \boldsymbol{L}^\top \boldsymbol{y}- \boldsymbol{\beta} \|^2|\ \boldsymbol{\beta},\sigma^2 ]\right] \quad \mbox{for any} \ \boldsymbol{L} \in \mathcal{M}(n,k).
\end{align}
The right-hand side of \eqref{MSE} equals 
\begin{align*}
\tr\left\{\gamma \boldsymbol{L}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}\boldsymbol{L}+ (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{L}-\boldsymbol{I}_k)^\top\boldsymbol{W}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{L}-\boldsymbol{I}_k)\right\}.
\end{align*}
We provide the explicit expression of $\boldsymbol{L}_* ^\top \boldsymbol{y}$ in Appendix~\ref{sec:A}.

In general, Bayes linear estimators are of the following form:
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K}) =  \boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top(\boldsymbol{\Phi}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}, \quad 
\boldsymbol{\Phi} \in \mathcal{S}^+(n), \ \boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^N(k).  
\end{align*}
The above-mentioned non-unbiased estimators for $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ in the general linear model are included in the extended Bayes class, where the extended Bayes class is the class that contains Bayes linear estimators and their limit points \citep{RefR76}. 
Moreover, a fractional rank estimator and a linearly restricted least squares estimator are included in the extended Bayes class \citep{RefL78}.
Additionally, the linear admissibility of Bayes linear estimators has been discussed in many studies.
For example, Bayes linear estimators are included in the class of admissible linear estimators characterized by \cite{RefC66} and \cite{RefS75}, and the class of admissible linear estimators is precisely the extended Bayes class \citep{RefR76}.
Similarly, \cite{RefL78} mentioned that all admissible linear estimators are included in the extended Bayes class. For linear admissibility, see, for example, \cite{RefBM88}; \cite{RefB89}; \cite{RefHM96}; \cite{RefGM04}.
In this paper, we revisit linear statistical inference with Bayes linear estimators.
In particular, we supplement the theoretical aspects that have remained underexplored, especially in the context of linear inference.

While, \cite{RefR75} stated the following general ridge estimators
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K}) =  (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} + \boldsymbol{K})^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{y}, \quad 
\boldsymbol{\Phi} \in \mathcal{S}^+(n), \ \boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^N(k).
\end{align*}
Hereafter, we consider that $\boldsymbol{\Phi} \in \{\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{\Omega}\}$.
For example, $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{0})$
corresponds to an ordinary least squares estimator, $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{0})$ to a generalized least squares estimator.
In particular, $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{0})$ is well known to be the best linear unbiased estimator (BLUE) in the general linear model.
General ridge estimators have several favorable properties, such as linear sufficiency, linear completeness, and admissibility in the class of linear estimators. 
In the theory of linear inference, properties such as linear sufficiency and admissibility in the class of linear estimators are of interest; see, for example, \cite{RefC66}, \cite{RefR76}, and \cite{RefD83}.
As shown by \cite{RefR76} and \cite{RefM96}, the general ridge estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$ is linearly sufficient and admissible within the class of linear estimators under the squared loss function, and linear estimators satisfying these properties simultaneously are also general ridge estimators.

Although it has not been well-studied, we are interested in the relationship between the Bayes linear estimator and the general ridge estimator.
When $\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, it holds that
\begin{align}\label{BG}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K}) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}).
\end{align}
In other words, the Bayes linear estimators share the properties of general ridge estimators in certain situations. 
However, a general ridge estimator is not necessarily a Bayes linear estimator, but can be exhibited as a limit of Bayes linear estimators \citep{RefR76}.
If $\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{+}(k)$, the Bayes linear estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$ is both linearly sufficient and linearly complete.
However, when $\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)$, the Bayes linear estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$  possesses linear completeness but does not necessarily have linear sufficiency. 
This fact will be discussed in Section~\ref{sec:3}.

In Section~\ref{sec:4}, for given $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)$, we derive a necessary and sufficient condition for the equality
\begin{align}\label{B2E}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2) \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n
\end{align}
to hold. 
Such a condition is of particular interest because when \eqref{B2E} holds, properties of $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1)$ such as linear completeness and linear admissibility are shared by $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2)$.
Furthermore, for given $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)$, the necessary and sufficient condition for the equality
\begin{align}\label{R2E}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2) \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n 
\end{align}
to hold have been derived by \cite{RefTK20} and
\cite{RefMT25}.
In the case \eqref{R2E} with $\boldsymbol{K}_1=\boldsymbol{K}_2=\boldsymbol{0}$, the equivalence conditions under which $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{0})$ equals $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{0})$, that is, $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{0})$ is BLUE, have been thoroughly discussed; see, for example, \cite{RefR67}, \cite{RefK68}, \cite{RefPS89} and references therein.
In this paper, we discuss an equivalent condition for \eqref{B2E} in more detail and provide proofs in two different approaches.

In Section~\ref{sec:5}, instead of looking for pairs $(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{X},\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2)$ such that two Bayes linear estimators coincide for all
$\boldsymbol{y}\in\mathbb{R}^n$ as \eqref{B2E}, 
we derive the set of $\boldsymbol{y}$ that satisfies
\begin{align}\label{B2Ey}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2)
\end{align}
for given $(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{X},\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2)$.
The equality \eqref{B2Ey} can be seen as an extension of \eqref{B2E}.
In previous studies, \cite{RefK80} has identified all $\boldsymbol{y}$ values such that $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{0})$ and $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{0})$ are equal.
Additionally, \cite{RefGT97} derived the conditions under which 
 the linear transformation 
$\boldsymbol{B}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{0})$ is equivalent to $\boldsymbol{B}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{0})$.
We try to identify the set of $\boldsymbol{y}$ that satisfies \eqref{B2Ey} for given $(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{X},\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2)$.

If we consider a quadratic estimator of $\sigma^2$, particularly when assuming a normal distribution of the observations, we often use the (generalized) residual sum of squares.
\cite{RefMT25} derived a necessary and sufficient condition for the following equality 
\begin{equation}\label{RSSG}
RSS_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1) = RSS_{GR}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2) \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{align*}
RSS_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K}) = \left\| \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1/2} \left( \boldsymbol{y} - \boldsymbol{X} \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K}) \right) \right\|^2, \quad
\boldsymbol{\Phi} \in \mathcal{S}^+(n), \ 
\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^N(k).     
\end{align*}
Also, \cite{RefTK20} yielded an equivalent condition such that
\begin{align*}
 \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2)\quad \mbox{and} \quad RSS_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1) = RSS_{GR}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2) \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n.
\end{align*}
In Section~\ref{sec:6}, we consider the following (generalized) residual sums of squares
\begin{align*}
RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K}) = \left\| \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1/2} \left( \boldsymbol{y} - \boldsymbol{X} \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K}) \right) \right\|^2, \quad
\boldsymbol{\Phi} \in \mathcal{S}^+(n), \ 
\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^N(k),  
\end{align*}
under Bayes linear estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K})$ and derive a necessary and sufficient condition for the equality
\begin{equation}\label{BRSE1}
RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1) = RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2) \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n 
\end{equation}
to hold. 
Additionally, a necessary and sufficient condition for the two equalities \eqref{B2E} and \eqref{BRSE1} to hold simultaneously is provided.

Section~\ref{sec:7} concludes the paper and outlines future directions.
\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:2}
In this section, we make preparations for some results used in subsequent sections.

First, we introduce a useful representation of $ \boldsymbol{\Omega}$  and $ \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}$.
\begin{lem}\label{OOI}
It holds that
\begin{equation}\label{Orep}
\boldsymbol{\Omega} 
= \left( \begin{matrix} \boldsymbol{X} & \boldsymbol{Z} \end{matrix}\right) \left(\begin{matrix} \boldsymbol{\Gamma} & \boldsymbol{\Xi} \\ \boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top & \boldsymbol{\Delta} \end{matrix} \right) \left( \begin{matrix} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \\ \boldsymbol{Z}^\top \end{matrix}\right)
\end{equation}
for some $\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, $\boldsymbol{\Xi} \in \mathcal{M}(k,n-k)$, and $\boldsymbol{\Delta} \in \mathcal{S}^+(n-k)$.
Moreover, when $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ is expressed as \eqref{Orep}, it holds that
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} 
= \begin{pmatrix} \boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} & \boldsymbol{Z}(\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1} \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{A} & \boldsymbol{B} \\ 
\boldsymbol{C} & \boldsymbol{D}
\end{pmatrix} 
\begin{pmatrix} (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \\ 
(\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1} \boldsymbol{Z}^\top
\end{pmatrix},    
\end{align*}
where
\begin{equation} \label{ABCD}
\boldsymbol{A} = (\boldsymbol{\Gamma} - \boldsymbol{\Xi} \boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top)^{-1}, \ 
\boldsymbol{B} = -\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{\Xi} \boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}, \
\boldsymbol{C} = \boldsymbol{B}^\top, \ and \
\boldsymbol{D} = \boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1} + \boldsymbol{B}^\top \boldsymbol{A}^{-1} \boldsymbol{B}.
\end{equation}
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
Let $\boldsymbol{G} = \begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X} & \boldsymbol{Z}
\end{pmatrix}\in \mathcal{G}\boldsymbol{\ell}(n)$ whose inverse is given by
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{G}^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix}
(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top\\
(\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Then, $\boldsymbol{\Omega}\in \mathcal{G}\boldsymbol{\ell}(n)$ can be expressed as
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\Omega} &= \boldsymbol{G}\boldsymbol{G}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}(\boldsymbol{G}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{G}^\top\\
& = \begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X} & \boldsymbol{Z}
\end{pmatrix}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top\\
(\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top
\end{pmatrix}\boldsymbol{\Omega}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} & \boldsymbol{Z}(\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}
\end{pmatrix}\right\}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X}^\top \\
\boldsymbol{Z}^\top
\end{pmatrix}\\
& = \begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X} & \boldsymbol{Z}
\end{pmatrix} 
\begin{pmatrix}
(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{X} (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} & (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{Z} (\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}\\
(\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} &  (\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1} \boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{Z} (\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X}^\top \\
\boldsymbol{Z}^\top
\end{pmatrix}\\
& =  \begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X} & \boldsymbol{Z}
\end{pmatrix} 
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\Gamma} & \boldsymbol{\Xi}\\
\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top & \boldsymbol{\Delta}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X}^\top \\
\boldsymbol{Z}^\top
\end{pmatrix} \quad (\say).
\end{align*}
Moreover, from the inverse formula for block matrices, we obtain
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\Gamma} & \boldsymbol{\Xi}\\
\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top & \boldsymbol{\Delta}
\end{pmatrix}^{-1}
= \begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{A} & - \boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{\Xi}\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1} \\
-\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top \boldsymbol{A} &\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top \boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{\Xi}\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Since
\begin{align*}
 \boldsymbol{C} = (-\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{\Xi}\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1})^\top = -\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top\boldsymbol{A}
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{B}^\top \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\boldsymbol{B}=(-\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top \boldsymbol{A})\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}(-\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{\Xi}\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1})=\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top \boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{\Xi}\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1},
\end{align*}
$\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}$ can be written as
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} &= 
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X}^\top \\
\boldsymbol{Z}^\top
\end{pmatrix}^{-1}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\Gamma} & \boldsymbol{\Xi}\\
\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top & \boldsymbol{\Delta}
\end{pmatrix}^{-1}
\begin{pmatrix} 
\boldsymbol{X} & \boldsymbol{Z}
\end{pmatrix}^{-1}\\
& = \begin{pmatrix} \boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} & \boldsymbol{Z}(\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1} \end{pmatrix} \left(\begin{matrix} \boldsymbol{A} & \boldsymbol{B} \\ \boldsymbol{C} & \boldsymbol{D}\end{matrix} \right) \begin{pmatrix} (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \\ (\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1} \boldsymbol{Z}^\top \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{rem}
In this paper, we refer to $\boldsymbol{\Gamma},\boldsymbol{\Xi}$, and $\boldsymbol{\Delta}$ as the ``covariance structure''.
The covariance structure plays a critical role in the main results presented in Sections~\ref{sec:4} and \ref{sec:6}.
\end{rem}
Next, we introduce the Woodbury formula (see, for example, \cite{RefG03}).
\begin{lem}\label{WE}
For positive integers $m_1$ and $m_2$, let $\boldsymbol{A}\in \mathcal{G}\boldsymbol{\ell}(m_1), \ \boldsymbol{B}\in \mathcal{M}(m_1,m_2), \ \boldsymbol{C}\in \mathcal{G}\boldsymbol{\ell}(m_2)$, and $\boldsymbol{D} \in \mathcal{M}(m_2,m_1)$. If $\boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{BCD}$ is nonsingular, then
\begin{align*}
    (\boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{BCD})^{-1}=\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\boldsymbol{B}(\boldsymbol{C}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{D}\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\boldsymbol{B})^{-1}\boldsymbol{D}\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}
\end{align*}
holds true.
\end{lem}
Now, let us prove \eqref{BG}.
\begin{prop}\label{Pr23}
If $\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^{+}(k)$, then \eqref{BG} holds.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Since $\boldsymbol{K}$ is nonsingular, it holds that
\begin{align*}
(\boldsymbol{\Phi}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1} = \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}
\end{align*}
from Lemma~\ref{WE}.
So, $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K}) $ can be rewritten as
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K})  &=\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top(\boldsymbol{\Phi}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}\\
&= \boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top\left\{\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\right\}\boldsymbol{y}\\
&=\left\{\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\right\}\boldsymbol{y}\\
&=\left\{\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{K}(\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}-\boldsymbol{K}^{-1})(\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\right\}\boldsymbol{y}\\
&=\left\{\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}+(\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\right\}\boldsymbol{y}\\
&=(\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}\\
& = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}).
\end{align*}
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
Furthermore, $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K})$ can be rewritten as follows. 
This fact is used in the proofs in Sections~\ref{sec:3} and \ref{sec:4}.
Although \eqref{BG} holds only when $\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, Lemma~\ref{ABLE} holds if $\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$. 
\begin{lem}\label{ABLE}
For a given $\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$, Bayes linear estimators $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K})$ can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{another}
     \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K})  = \boldsymbol{K}\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\right\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{y}. 
    \end{align}
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
To demonstrate \eqref{another} for any $\boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$, it is sufficient to prove the following equality:
\begin{align*}
     \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}  = \boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top +\boldsymbol{\Phi} )^{-1}.
    \end{align*}
    Then, it holds that
    \begin{align*}
     & \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top +\boldsymbol{\Phi} )\\
     = & \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top  \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top +\boldsymbol{X}^\top )\\
     = & \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K} + \boldsymbol{I}_n )\boldsymbol{X}^\top\\
     = &  \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1}\{\boldsymbol{K}+(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\}\boldsymbol{X}^\top\\
     = & \boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top.
    \end{align*}
    This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
The definitions of linear sufficiency and linear completeness are introduced by \cite{RefD83} and \cite{RefM87}.
A useful lemma that will be utilized in the proof in Section~\ref{sec:3} has been summarized by \cite{RefM96}.
\begin{lem}[\cite{RefM96}]\label{M96}
Let $\boldsymbol{T}= \boldsymbol{\Omega} + \boldsymbol{XKX}^\top$.
Then, \\
(i) $\boldsymbol{Fy}$ is linearly sufficient if and only if $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X}) \subseteq \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{F}^\top)$.\\
(ii) $\boldsymbol{Fy}$ is linearly complete if and only if $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{F\Omega}) \subseteq \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{FX})$.
\end{lem}
\begin{rem}
The conditions $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X}) \subseteq \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{F}^\top),\ \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{F\Omega}) \subseteq \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{FX})$ of Lemma~\ref{M96} are equivalent to $\boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{F}^\top\boldsymbol{G} \ \ \mbox{for some matrix} \ \ \boldsymbol{G},$ and \ $\boldsymbol{F\Omega} = \boldsymbol{FXH} \ \ \mbox{for some matrix} \ \ \boldsymbol{H}$, respectively.
\end{rem}
The following lemma will be used to derive the necessary and sufficient condition for \eqref{B2E}.
\begin{lem}\label{AAA}
For given $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)$, the equality \eqref{B2E} holds if and only if 
\begin{align}\label{B2E2}
\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2) \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n.
\end{align}
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
Since $(\boldsymbol{X}\quad \boldsymbol{Z})$
is nonsingular, \eqref{B2E2} is equivalent to
\begin{align*}
&\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X} & \boldsymbol{Z}
\end{pmatrix}^\top
\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) =
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{X} & \boldsymbol{Z}    
\end{pmatrix}^\top
\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2).
\end{align*}
Moreover, since the equality $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{Z}=\boldsymbol{0}$ holds and $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}$ is nonsingular, this condition is rewritten as
\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2)\\
 \Leftrightarrow \quad & \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2)
\end{align*}
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
Additionally, we introduce the following lemma about projection matrices which will be used in Section~\ref{sec:4}; see, for example, \cite{RefG03}.
\begin{lem}\label{AABB}
Let $\boldsymbol{A}\in \mathcal{M}(m,n), \ \boldsymbol{B}\in \mathcal{M}(m,p) $. 
Then $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{A}) = \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{B})$ if and only if the identity
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{A}^+ = \boldsymbol{B}\boldsymbol{B}^+
\end{align*}
is satisfied.
\end{lem}
We calculate the expectation vector of $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K})$.
This lemma will be used in the proof in Section~\ref{sec:4}.
\begin{lem}\label{EAV}
The expectation vector of $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K})$ can be calculated as
\begin{align*}
\E\left[\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K})\right]=\boldsymbol{K}(\boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{K})^{-1}\boldsymbol{\beta},
\end{align*}
where $\boldsymbol{A} = (\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}$.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
From Lemma~\ref{ABLE}, $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K})$ is rewritten as
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K})  = \boldsymbol{K}\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\right\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{y} .
\end{align*}
Then, the expectation vector of $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K})$ can be calculated as
\begin{align*}
\E\left[\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K})\right] 
&=  \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \E\left[\boldsymbol{y}\right] \\
&=  \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\beta}\\ 
&= \boldsymbol{K}(\boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{K})^{-1}\boldsymbol{\beta}.
\end{align*}
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{rem}\label{remB}
From Lemma~\ref{EAV}, it can be seen that  Bayes linear estimators $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K})$ are not unbiased estimators, regardless of the choice of $\boldsymbol{\Phi}$ and $\boldsymbol{K}$.
Hence, the Bayes linear estimators $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K})$ cannot be expressed as the weighted least squares estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{0})$. 
However, this unbiased estimator can be represented as a limit of Bayes linear estimators, that is,
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, a^{-1}\boldsymbol{I}_k) \rightarrow \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{0}) \quad \as \ a \downarrow 0.
\end{align*}
\end{rem}
Finally, we present a technical lemma that will be used in Section~\ref{sec:5}.
\begin{lem}\label{lem43}
Let $\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)$.
Then it holds that
\begin{align*}
\rk(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XKX}^\top) = \rk(\boldsymbol{\Omega} \quad \boldsymbol{X}).
\end{align*}
Additionally, the following statements are equivalent:\vspace{1ex}\\
(i) $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X}) \subseteq \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XKX}^\top)$.\\
(ii) $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XKX}^\top) = \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{\Omega} \quad \boldsymbol{X})$.\\ 
(iii) $\rk(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XKX}^\top) = \rk(\boldsymbol{\Omega} \quad \boldsymbol{X})$.
\end{lem}
\section{Properties of Bayes linear estimator} \label{sec:3}
In this section, we discuss the properties of the Bayes linear estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$, particularly its linear sufficiency and linear completeness by using Lemma~\ref{M96}.
The following theorem is the first main result of this paper.
\begin{thm}\label{LSAC}
(i) For given $\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$ is  linearly sufficient.\\
(ii) For given $\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$, $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$ is linearly complete.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
(i) Let $\boldsymbol{T}= \boldsymbol{\Omega} + \boldsymbol{XKX}^\top$ and $\boldsymbol{F}=\boldsymbol{KX}^\top \boldsymbol{T}^{-1}$.
For the Bayes linear estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})=\boldsymbol{Fy}$, $\boldsymbol{TF}^\top$ is calculated as
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{TF}^\top = \boldsymbol{TT}^{-1} \boldsymbol{XK} = \boldsymbol{XK}.
\end{align*}
Let us denote $\boldsymbol{G}=\boldsymbol{K}^{-1}$.
Since $\boldsymbol{K}$ is nonsingular, the following equality holds:
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{F}^\top\boldsymbol{G}.
\end{align*}
Hence, $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X}) \subseteq \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{TF}^\top)$ is satisfied.
Therefore, it follows from Lemma~\ref{M96} that $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$ is linearly sufficient.\\
(ii) Let
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{F}= \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}.
\end{align*}
From Lemma~\ref{ABLE}, the Bayes linear estimator is rewritten as $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})=\boldsymbol{Fy}$.
Moreover, $\boldsymbol{F\Omega}$ and $\boldsymbol{FX}$ are calculated as
\begin{align*}
& \boldsymbol{F\Omega} = \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top,\\
& \boldsymbol{FX} = \boldsymbol{K}\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\}^{-1},
\end{align*}
respectively.
Let us denote $\boldsymbol{H}=(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top$. 
Then, the following equality holds:
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{F\Omega} = \boldsymbol{FXH}.
\end{align*} 
Hence, $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{F\Omega}) \subseteq \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{FX})$ is satisfied. 
Therefore, it follows from Lemma~\ref{M96} that $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$ is linearly complete.
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{rem}
When $\boldsymbol{K} \notin \mathcal{S}^{+}(k)$, the Bayes linear estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$
possesses linear completeness but lacks linear sufficiency.
In contrast, the general ridge estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K})$ possesses both linear completeness and linear sufficiency.
\end{rem}
\section{Equality between two Bayes linear estimators for arbitrary \texorpdfstring{$\boldsymbol{y}$}{y}}
\label{sec:4}
In this section, we derive the necessary and sufficient condition for the equality \eqref{B2E} to hold.
The following theorem is the second main result of this paper.
There are two approaches to prove this theorem.
The first approach utilizes Lemma~\ref{ABLE}, while the second employs Lemma~\ref{AAA}, both yielding the same results.
\begin{thm}\label{thm41}
For given $\boldsymbol{K}_1, \boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$, the equality \eqref{B2E} holds if and only if both of the following conditions hold in \eqref{Orep}:
\begin{align}
&\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\Gamma} = \boldsymbol{K}_1, \label{411}\\
&\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Xi}=\boldsymbol{0}. \label{412}
\end{align} 
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
For simplicity, $\boldsymbol{A}$ and $\boldsymbol{B}$ are defined as \eqref{ABCD}. 
From Lemma~\ref{ABLE}, the equality \eqref{B2E} can be written as
\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{K}_1\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_1\right\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} = \boldsymbol{K}_2\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top  \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad  & \boldsymbol{K}_1(\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_1)^{-1}\boldsymbol{A}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} 
= \boldsymbol{K}_2\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top  \boldsymbol{X} )^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top,
\end{align*}
which is further equivalent to the following two equations:
\begin{align}\label{1T}
\boldsymbol{K}_1(\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_1)^{-1}
= \boldsymbol{K}_2\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1}
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}\label{2T}
\boldsymbol{K}_1(\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_1)^{-1}\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\boldsymbol{B} = \boldsymbol{0}
\end{align}
since $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{Z}=\boldsymbol{0}$ and $(\boldsymbol{X} \quad \boldsymbol{Z})$ is nonsingular. Moreover, under \eqref{1T}, \eqref{2T} can be rewritten as follows:
\begin{align}\label{3T}
&\boldsymbol{K}_2\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1} \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\boldsymbol{B} =\boldsymbol{0} \notag \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & -\boldsymbol{K}_2\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1} \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{\Xi} \boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1} =\boldsymbol{0}\notag \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{K}_2\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\Xi} =\boldsymbol{0}\notag \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2-(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\right\}\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\Xi} =\boldsymbol{0}\notag \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \left[\boldsymbol{I}_k - (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1}\right] \boldsymbol{\Xi} =\boldsymbol{0}\notag \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \left\{\boldsymbol{I}_k - (\boldsymbol{I}_k+\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\right\} \boldsymbol{\Xi} =\boldsymbol{0}\notag \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \left\{(\boldsymbol{I}_k+\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}) - \boldsymbol{I}_k\right\} \boldsymbol{\Xi} =\boldsymbol{0}\notag \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Xi}=\boldsymbol{0}.
\end{align}
Additionally, \eqref{1T} can be expressed as
\allowdisplaybreaks\begin{align*}
&(\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_1-\boldsymbol{A}^{-1})(\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_1)^{-1} = \left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2-(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\right\}\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{I}_k - \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_1)^{-1} = \boldsymbol{I}_k - (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}^{-1}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &  (\boldsymbol{I}_k+\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{A})^{-1} = (\boldsymbol{I}_k +\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &  \boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{A} =\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &  \boldsymbol{K}_1=\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}-\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Xi}\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &  \boldsymbol{K}_1=\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}
\end{align*}
under \eqref{3T}. This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{rem}\label{rem5}
Theorem~\ref{thm41} can also be proven from Lemma~\ref{AAA}.
See the following proof.
\end{rem}
\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm41}]
From  Lemma~\ref{AAA}, the equality \eqref{B2E} is equivalent to \eqref{B2E2}, which is further written as
\begin{align}\label{4T}
& \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}=\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1} \notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}-\boldsymbol{\Omega})(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}=(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}-\boldsymbol{I}_n)(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}\notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{I}_n-\boldsymbol{\Omega}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}=\boldsymbol{I}_n-(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}\notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &\boldsymbol{\Omega}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}=(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}\notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})\boldsymbol{\Omega}=\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega}=\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}.
\end{align}
In addition, the equality \eqref{4T} holds if and only if the following four equalities simultaneously hold:
\begin{align}
& \boldsymbol{X}^\top  \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}, \label{XX}\\
&  \boldsymbol{X}^\top  \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{Z} = \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{Z}, \label{XZ}\\
& \boldsymbol{Z}^\top  \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}, \label{ZX}\\
& \boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{Z}=\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{Z}, \label{ZZ}
\end{align}
since $(\boldsymbol{X} \quad \boldsymbol{Z})$ is nonsingular. 
Moreover, \eqref{ZX} and \eqref{ZZ} are satisfied by $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{Z}=\boldsymbol{0}$. 
Therefore, these conditions can be reduced to \eqref{XX} and \eqref{XZ}. 
Additionally, \eqref{XX} and \eqref{XZ} are rewritten as 
\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\Gamma} \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top  \boldsymbol{X}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\Gamma} = \boldsymbol{K}_1,
\end{align*}
and 
\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\Xi} \boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\Xi}  = \boldsymbol{0}
\end{align*}
by using the notation \eqref{Orep}.
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{rem}
Based on the result of Theorem~\ref{thm41}, to examine the equivalence, it suffices to identify the covariance structure $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$ and $\boldsymbol{\Xi}$; $\boldsymbol{\Delta}$ may remain unknown. 
See Example~\ref{Ex1} below.
\end{rem}
As stated in Section~\ref{sec:1}, \cite{RefTK20} derived the necessary and sufficient condition for the equivalence of two generalized ridge estimators. Comparing their results with Theorem~\ref{thm41} provides us with further insights.
\begin{rem}
\cite{RefTK20} showed that the equality \eqref{R2E} holds if and only if both of the following conditions hold in \eqref{Orep}:
\begin{align}
& \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\Gamma} \boldsymbol{K}_1 =\boldsymbol{K}_2, \label{gre1}\\
&\boldsymbol{\Xi}=\boldsymbol{0}, \label{gre2}
\end{align} 
both of which $\boldsymbol{K}_1$ and $\boldsymbol{K}_2$ are given.
Clearly, the results of Theorem~\ref{thm41} and theirs are independent of $\boldsymbol{\Delta}$.
On the other hand, we find that
\eqref{412} is a necessary condition on $\boldsymbol{\Xi}$ for \eqref{gre2}.
\end{rem}
Let us demonstrate an example that satisfies \eqref{B2E}.
\begin{exam}[example for Theorem~\ref{thm41}]\label{Ex1}
Let
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{X}=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1 \\
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix},
\
\boldsymbol{\Omega} =
\begin{pmatrix}
6+a & -3-a & 4+2a \\
-3-a & 6+a & -4-2a \\
4+2a & -4-2a & 4a 
\end{pmatrix}, \ 
\boldsymbol{K}_1=
\begin{pmatrix}
3 & 3 \\
3 & 3 
\end{pmatrix},
\ \mbox{and}\ \,
\boldsymbol{K}_2= \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 1 \\
1 & 1 
\end{pmatrix},
\end{align*}
where $a$ is an arbitrary positive constant.
First, let $\boldsymbol{Z},\ \boldsymbol{\Gamma}, \ \boldsymbol{\Xi}$, and $\boldsymbol{\Delta}$ be selected as
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{Z} =
\begin{pmatrix}
1\\
-1\\
2
\end{pmatrix}, \
\boldsymbol{\Gamma} =
\begin{pmatrix}
2 & 1 \\
1 & 2
\end{pmatrix}, \
\boldsymbol{\Xi} =
\begin{pmatrix}
2\\
-2
\end{pmatrix}, \ \mbox{and}\ \,
\boldsymbol{\Delta} = a,
\end{align*}
then $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ can be written in the form of \eqref{Orep}. Also, it follows from $\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{I}_2$ that
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma} 
= \boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{\Gamma} 
= \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 1 \\
1 & 1 
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
2 & 1 \\
1 & 2
\end{pmatrix}
=\boldsymbol{K}_2,
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Xi} 
= \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{\Xi}
=\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 1 \\
1 & 1 
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
2\\
-2
\end{pmatrix}
= \boldsymbol{0}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, both \eqref{411} and \eqref{412} are satisfied.
In other words, the equality 
\[ \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_3, \boldsymbol{K}_2)\left(=\cfrac{1}{3}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 1 & 0\\
1 & 1 & 0
\end{pmatrix}\boldsymbol{y}\right) \]
holds for any $\boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^{3}$. 
\end{exam}
Next, we clarify when there exists $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$ satisfying the condition \eqref{411} for given $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2\in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$.
To simplify the notation, let
\begin{align*}
& \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_i =  (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{1/2}\boldsymbol{K}_i(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{1/2}\quad (i=1,2),\\
& \Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}} = (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{1/2}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{1/2}.
\end{align*}
\begin{prop}\label{pr42}
There exists $\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$ satisfying \eqref{411} if and only if $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1$ and $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2$ satisfy
\begin{align}\label{K1K2}
\mathcal{C}(\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1) = \mathcal{C}(\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)
\quad \an \quad \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2 = \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2 \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1.
\end{align}
In this case, $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$ is of the form 
\begin{align}\label{gamma}
\boldsymbol{\Gamma} = (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1/2}\left\{\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1+ (\boldsymbol{I}_k-\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)\boldsymbol{H}(\boldsymbol{I}_k-\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)\right\}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1/2} \notag \\ 
\fs \ \boldsymbol{H} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k).
\end{align}
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Firstly, note that \eqref{411} is equivalent to 
\begin{align}\label{Kgamma1}
\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2  \Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}} = \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1.  
\end{align}
To see the necessity, assume that \eqref{411} is satisfied.
Since $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1$ is symmetric, it holds that
\begin{align}\label{Kgamma2}
\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2  \Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}} = \Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2.
\end{align}
So, the two matrices $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2$ and $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}$ commute.
Moreover, since $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}$ is nonsingular, we have $\rk(\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1) = \rk(\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)$, and hence $\mathcal{C}(\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1) = \mathcal{C}(\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)$.
Hereafter, we write $\rk(\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1) = \rk(\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)=r$.
Then, $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2$ can be decomposed as $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2 = \boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_2\boldsymbol{V}^\top$
with a diagonal matrix $\boldsymbol{D}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^+(r)$ and $\boldsymbol{V}\in \mathcal{M}(k,r)$.
From \eqref{Kgamma2}, $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}$ can be rewritten as
$\Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}} = \boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{F}\boldsymbol{V}^\top + \boldsymbol{W}\boldsymbol{G}\boldsymbol{W}^\top$
for some $\boldsymbol{F} \in \mathcal{S}^+(r),\ \boldsymbol{G} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k-r)$, both of which are diagonal matrices.
Since $(\boldsymbol{V} \quad \boldsymbol{W})$ is an orthogonal matrix, it holds that
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{V}^\top \boldsymbol{V} = \boldsymbol{I}_r, \ \boldsymbol{V}^\top \boldsymbol{W} = \boldsymbol{0}, \ \boldsymbol{W}^\top \boldsymbol{W} = \boldsymbol{I}_{k-r}, \ \an \ \ \boldsymbol{W} \boldsymbol{W}^\top =  \boldsymbol{I}_k - \boldsymbol{V} \boldsymbol{V}^\top.
\end{align*}
Hence, it follows from \eqref{Kgamma1} that
\begin{align*}
\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1 =  (\boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_2\boldsymbol{V}^\top)(\boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{F}\boldsymbol{V}^\top + \boldsymbol{W}\boldsymbol{G}\boldsymbol{W}^\top)
= \boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_2\boldsymbol{F}\boldsymbol{V}^\top 
= \boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_1\boldsymbol{V}^\top \quad (\say).
\end{align*}
Note that $\boldsymbol{D}_1$ must be also diagonal, which implies that $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1$ and $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2$ commute.
Therefore, we obtain \eqref{K1K2} and
\begin{align*}
\Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}} &= \boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_2^{-1}\boldsymbol{D}_1\boldsymbol{V}^\top + \boldsymbol{W}\boldsymbol{G}\boldsymbol{W}^\top \\
& = \boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_2^{-1}\boldsymbol{V}^\top \boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_1\boldsymbol{V}^\top + \boldsymbol{W}\boldsymbol{W}^\top \boldsymbol{W}\boldsymbol{G}\boldsymbol{W}^\top\boldsymbol{W}\boldsymbol{W}^\top \\
& = (\boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_2^{-1}\boldsymbol{V}^\top)(\boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_1\boldsymbol{V}^\top) + \boldsymbol{W}\boldsymbol{W}^\top \boldsymbol{H}\boldsymbol{W}\boldsymbol{W}^\top \ \fs \ \boldsymbol{H} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k),
\end{align*}
where the last expression is equivalent to \eqref{gamma}, since $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+ = \boldsymbol{V}\boldsymbol{D}_2^{-1}\boldsymbol{V}^\top$ and $ \boldsymbol{W}\boldsymbol{W}^\top 
= \boldsymbol{I}_k - \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2$.
Conversely, suppose that \eqref{K1K2} holds. 
Note that $\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1^+=\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+$ from Lemma~\ref{AABB}.
When we choose $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$ as \eqref{gamma}, it holds that
\begin{align*}
\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2 \Tilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}
& = \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2 \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+ \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1 + \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2 (\boldsymbol{I}_k-\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+ \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)\boldsymbol{H}(\boldsymbol{I}_k-\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+ \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)\\
& = \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2 \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+ \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1\\
& = \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1^+\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1 \\
& = \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1.
\end{align*}
This shows the existence of $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$ that satisfies \eqref{Kgamma1}, which is equivalent to \eqref{411}.
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{rem}
The choice of $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$ in Example~\ref{Ex1} is based on Proposition~\ref{pr42}.
It follows from $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{I}_2$ and 
$\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+ = \cfrac{1}{4}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 1\\
1 & 1
\end{pmatrix}$
that
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\Gamma} &= \Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_1+ (\boldsymbol{I}_k-\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)\boldsymbol{H}(\boldsymbol{I}_k-\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2^+\Tilde{\boldsymbol{K}}_2)\\
& = \cfrac{3}{2}\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 1\\
1 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
+ \cfrac{1}{4}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & -1 \\
-1 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{H}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & -1 \\
-1 & 1
\end{pmatrix} \ \fs \ \boldsymbol{H} \in \mathcal{S}^+(2).
\end{align*}
Especially, letting $\boldsymbol{H} = \boldsymbol{I}_2$ gives $\boldsymbol{\Gamma} = \begin{pmatrix}
2 & 1\\
1& 2
\end{pmatrix}$. 
\end{rem}
If $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, we directly obtain the following corollary to Theorem~\ref{thm41}, utilizing Proposition~\ref{Pr23} and results from \cite{RefMT25} (Corollary 3.2).
\begin{cor}\label{cr42}
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)]
For given $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, the equality \eqref{B2E} holds if and only if
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\Omega}=\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{K}_2^{-1}\boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top + \boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\Delta}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \quad \mbox{for some} \ \ \boldsymbol{\Delta} \in \mathcal{S}^{+}(n-k),
\end{align*}
which is also equivalent to  $\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{\Omega X }\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{K}_1^{-1}$.
\item[(ii)]
For given $\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, the equality \eqref{B2E} with
$\boldsymbol{K}_1 = \boldsymbol{K}_2 = \boldsymbol{K}$ holds if and only if 
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\Omega}=\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top + \boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\Delta}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \quad \mbox{for some} \ \ \boldsymbol{\Delta} \in \mathcal{S}^{+}(n-k),
\end{align*}
which is also equivalent to $\boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{\Omega X}$.
\end{itemize}  
\end{cor}
\begin{rem}
Let $\lambda$ be a positive constant. 
Then, Corollary~\ref{cr42} implies that the equality between ordinary ridge estimators
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \lambda^{-1}\boldsymbol{I}_k) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \lambda^{-1}\boldsymbol{I}_k) \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n 
\end{align*}
holds if and only if $\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{\Omega X}$.
\end{rem}
The following corollary presents a necessary and sufficient condition for the equality between two classes of Bayes linear estimators.
\begin{prop}
Consider the classes 
$\{\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K})\}_{\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)}$ and $\{\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K})\}_{\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)}$ of Bayes linear estimators. The equality 
\begin{align}\label{CE}
\{\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K})\}_{\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)} = \{\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K})\}_{\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)}
\end{align}
holds if and only if $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ is of the form
\begin{align}\label{413}
\boldsymbol{\Omega} = \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\boldsymbol{X}^\top +\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\Delta}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top
\end{align}
for some $\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \in \mathcal{S}^{+}(k)$ and $\boldsymbol{\Delta} \in \mathcal{S}^{+}(n-k)$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Note that \eqref{413} is equivalent to $\boldsymbol{\Xi} =\boldsymbol{0}$ in \eqref{Orep}.
To see the sufficiency, suppose that \eqref{413} is satisfied. 
Then, Theorem~\ref{thm41} guarantees that for each $\boldsymbol{K}$, the equality $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K})$ holds. Since $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$ is nonsingular, we have \eqref{CE}. 
Next, we see the necessity. 
If \eqref{CE} holds, then $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{I}_k) \in \{\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K})\}_{\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)}$, which means there exists a $\boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)$ such that
$\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{I}_k) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2)$. Since it follows from \eqref{411} that $\boldsymbol{K}_2$ should be $(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\Gamma})^{-1}$ by using Theorem~\ref{thm41}, $\boldsymbol{\Xi} =\boldsymbol{0}$ holds from \eqref{412}. 
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{rem}
The covariance structure \eqref{413} is a celebrated condition for the equivalence between $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{0})$ and $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR} (\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{0})$, and it has been studied extensively as explained in Section~\ref{sec:1}. 
Our result provides a new interpretation of this condition from a different perspective.
\end{rem}
\begin{rem}
\cite{RefTK20} showed that the necessary and sufficient condition for the equivalence between two classes of general ridge estimators, that is,
\begin{align}\label{CG}
\{\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K})\}_{\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)} = \{\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{GR}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K})\}_{\boldsymbol{K}\in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)}
\end{align}
is given by \eqref{413}. 
In other words, \eqref{CE} is equivalent to \eqref{CG}.
\end{rem}
The bias of the Bayes linear estimator $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi},\boldsymbol{K})$
\begin{align*}
\E\left[\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Phi}, \boldsymbol{K})\right]-\boldsymbol{\beta} = \boldsymbol{K}\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}\right\}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\beta}-\boldsymbol{\beta} = -(\boldsymbol{I}_k+\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Phi}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{\beta}
\end{align*}
is not zero, except for the case $\boldsymbol{\beta} = \boldsymbol{0}$. 
Moreover, we discuss the condition under which the biases of the two Bayes linear estimators $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1)$ and $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)$ coincide, that is, 
\begin{equation}\label{biascond}
\E\left[ \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) \right]
= \E\left[\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2)\right]
\quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{\beta} \in \mathbb{R}^k.
\end{equation}
If \eqref{B2E} holds, then \eqref{biascond} clearly holds. 
However, the converse is not generally true. 
First, we derive the necessary and sufficient condition under which \eqref{biascond} holds.
\begin{prop}\label{prbias}
Let $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ be expressed as \eqref{Orep}.
For given $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$, the equality \eqref{biascond} holds if and only if \eqref{1T}.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
It follows from Lemma~\ref{EAV}.  
\end{proof}  
Using Proposition~\ref{prbias}, we see that the necessary and sufficient condition for \eqref{B2E} to hold is given by \eqref{412} and the equivalence of biases. 
\begin{prop}
For given $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$, the equality \eqref{B2E} holds if and only if both \eqref{412} and  \eqref{biascond} hold.
\end{prop}
\section{Set of \texorpdfstring{$\boldsymbol{y}$}{y} satisfying the equality \eqref{B2Ey}}\label{sec:5}
In this section, we aim to characterize the set of $\boldsymbol{y}$ for which \eqref{B2Ey} is satisfied.
To simplify the notation, let us denote
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{M} = (\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{\Omega}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}-\boldsymbol{I}_n.
\end{align*}
The following theorem is the third main result of this paper.
\begin{thm}\label{thm51}
For given $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^{N}(k)$, the equality \eqref{B2Ey} holds if and only if 
\begin{align}\label{null}
\boldsymbol{y} \in \mathcal{N}[(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}].
\end{align}
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
To see the necessity, suppose that the equality \eqref{B2Ey} holds.  
This formula can be rewritten as follows:
\begin{align}\label{52}
&\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2) \notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) = \boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL} (\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2) \notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y} = \boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}\notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \{\boldsymbol{I}_n-\boldsymbol{\Omega}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\}\boldsymbol{y} = \{\boldsymbol{I}_n-(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\}\boldsymbol{y}\notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{\Omega}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y} = (\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}\notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{My} = \boldsymbol{0}.
\end{align}
Note that $\boldsymbol{y}\in \mathbb{R}^n = \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}\quad \boldsymbol{X})$, $\boldsymbol{y}$ can be expressed as 
\begin{align}\label{y}
\boldsymbol{y} = (\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{x} \quad \fs \ \ \boldsymbol{x}\in \mathbb{R}^n
\end{align}
from Lemma~\ref{lem43}.
By substituting \eqref{y} into \eqref{52}, we yield
\begin{align*}
&\{(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{\Omega}-(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\}\boldsymbol{x} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{x} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{x} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & (\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top) (\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathcal{N}[(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}].
\end{align*}
Conversely, suppose that \eqref{null} is satisfied. 
Note that $\boldsymbol{y}\in \mathbb{R}^n$ can be written as \eqref{y} and 
$(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{x} = \boldsymbol{0}$.
Then the Bayes linear estimators $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1)$ and $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2)$ can be calculated as
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1) & = \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}
= \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{x}
= \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}\boldsymbol{x},
\end{align*}
and 
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \boldsymbol{K}_2) &= \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{I}_n+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{I}_n+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1} (\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top) \boldsymbol{x}\\
& =  \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{I}_n+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega})\boldsymbol{x}\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{x},
\end{align*}
respectively. 
Thus, we have \eqref{B2Ey}.
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{rem}
Theorem~\ref{thm41} can be derived from Theorem~\ref{thm51}.
In fact, it holds that
\begin{align*}
&\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2) \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathcal{N}[(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}] \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & (\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{y} =\boldsymbol{0} \quad \mbox{for any} \ \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & (\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1} =\boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} = \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} = \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top
\end{align*}
and we have \eqref{4T}.
For the subsequent proof, refer to Remark~\ref{rem5}.
\end{rem}
Next, we consider Theorem~\ref{thm51} in the case where $\boldsymbol{K}_1 = \boldsymbol{K}_2 = \boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$.
\begin{cor}\label{cor52}
For given $\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, the equality \eqref{B2Ey} holds if and only if 
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{y} \in \mathcal{N}[\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{I}_n)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}].
\end{align*}
\end{cor}
\begin{rem}
Let $\lambda$ be a positive constant. 
Corollary~\ref{cor52} implies that the equality between ordinary ridge estimators
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \lambda^{-1}\boldsymbol{I}_k) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \lambda^{-1}\boldsymbol{I}_k) 
\end{align*}
holds if and only if 
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{y} \in \mathcal{N}[\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{I}_n)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \lambda^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}].
\end{align*}
\end{rem}
\newpage
Under certain conditions, the set of $\boldsymbol{y}$ that satisfies \eqref{B2Ey} can be expressed as a direct sum.
\begin{thm}\label{thm52}
Let $\boldsymbol{K}_1, \boldsymbol{K}_2 \in  \mathcal{S}^{+}(k)$ and $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ be expressed as $\boldsymbol{\Omega} = \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\boldsymbol{X}^\top + \boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\Delta} \boldsymbol{Z}^\top$. 
Then the equality \eqref{B2Ey} holds if and only if
\begin{align}\label{oplus}
\boldsymbol{y} \in \left\{\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X})\ \cap \ \mathcal{N}[(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}]\right\}  \oplus \mathcal{C}^\bot(\boldsymbol{X}).
\end{align}
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
To see the necessity, suppose that \eqref{B2Ey} is satisfied.
Then, \eqref{B2Ey} is equivalent to
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{M} \boldsymbol{y}  = \boldsymbol{0}.
\end{align*}
Since it follows from Lemma~\ref{WE} that
\begin{align*}
(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1} = \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} - \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_1^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1},
\end{align*}
$\boldsymbol{M}$ can be rewritten as
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{M} &= (\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{\Omega}\left\{\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} - \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_1^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\right\}-\boldsymbol{I}_n\\
& = \boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top - (\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_1^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{I}_n\\
& = \boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top - (\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_1^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top.
\end{align*}
Note that since $\boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n = \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X})\oplus\mathcal{C}^{\bot}(\boldsymbol{X})$, $\boldsymbol{y}$ can be written as
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{y} = \boldsymbol{a} + \boldsymbol{b},\quad \boldsymbol{a} \in \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X}), \ \boldsymbol{b} \in \mathcal{C}^{\bot}(\boldsymbol{X}).
\end{align*}
Furthermore, from Lemma~\ref{lem43}, the vector $\boldsymbol{a}$ can be expressed as
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{a} = (\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{c} \quad \fs \ \ \boldsymbol{c}\in \mathbb{R}^n
\end{align*}
and it holds that $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{b} = \boldsymbol{0}$ since $\boldsymbol{b} \in \mathcal{C}^{\bot}(\boldsymbol{X}) = \mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top)$.
Thus, $\boldsymbol{Ma},\boldsymbol{Mb}$ can be calculated as
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{Ma} & = \left\{(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{\Omega}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}-\boldsymbol{I}_n\right\}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{c}\\
& = \left\{(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{\Omega}-(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\right\}\boldsymbol{c}\\
& = (\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{c},\\
\boldsymbol{Mb} 
& = \left\{\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top - (\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_1^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top\right\}\boldsymbol{b} = \boldsymbol{0},
\end{align*}
respectively, and we have
\allowdisplaybreaks\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{My} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & (\boldsymbol{XK}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{c} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{c} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & (\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{c} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & (\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}\boldsymbol{a} = \boldsymbol{0}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{a} \in \mathcal{N}[(\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}].
\end{align*}
Conversely, suppose that \eqref{oplus} is satisfied
Then, $\boldsymbol{y}$ can be written as
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{y} = \boldsymbol{a} + \boldsymbol{b},\quad \boldsymbol{a} \in \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X}) \ \cap \ \mathcal{N}[(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}] , \ \boldsymbol{b} \in \mathcal{C}^{\bot}(\boldsymbol{X}).
\end{align*}
In this case,  the vector $\boldsymbol{a}$ is
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{a} = (\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{XK}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{c} \quad \fs \ \ \boldsymbol{c}\in \mathbb{R}^n
\end{align*}
from Lemma~\ref{lem43}.
Since $\boldsymbol{a} \in \mathcal{N}[(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}]$, it follows that
\begin{align*}
(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top) \boldsymbol{c}  = \boldsymbol{0}.
\end{align*}
Moreover, $\boldsymbol{b} \in \mathcal{C}^{\bot}(\boldsymbol{X}) = \mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top)$ implies that $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{b} = \boldsymbol{0}$.
So, the estimators $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1)$ and $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)$ can be calculated as follows:
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1)
& = \boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1} (\boldsymbol{a}+\boldsymbol{b})\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{c} + \boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top \left\{\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} - \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_1^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\right\}\boldsymbol{b}\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{c} + \boldsymbol{K}_1 \left\{\boldsymbol{I}_k - \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_1^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\right\}\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{b}\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{c} + \boldsymbol{K}_1 \left\{\boldsymbol{I}_k - \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{K}_1^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\right\}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{b}\\
& =  \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{c}\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{\Omega}\boldsymbol{c},
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)
& = \boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{I}_n+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1} (\boldsymbol{a}+\boldsymbol{b})\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{I}_n+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{c} + \boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \left\{\boldsymbol{I}_n - \boldsymbol{X} (\boldsymbol{K}_2^{-1}+\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^\top\right\}\boldsymbol{b}\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{I}_n+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega})\boldsymbol{c}\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{I}_n+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}(\boldsymbol{I}_n+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top)\boldsymbol{\Omega c}\\
& = \boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega c}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, we have \eqref{B2Ey}.
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{rem}
Let $\boldsymbol{K}_1, \boldsymbol{K}_2 \in  \mathcal{S}^{+}(k)$ and $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ be expressed as $\boldsymbol{\Omega} = \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\boldsymbol{X}^\top + \boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\Delta} \boldsymbol{Z}^\top$. 
Then the equality \eqref{B2E} holds if and only if
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X}) \subseteq \mathcal{N}[(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}].
\end{align*}
This proof can be carried out by using the decomposition $\mathbb{R}^n = \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X})\oplus\mathcal{C}^\bot(\boldsymbol{X})$.
\end{rem}
\begin{rem}
\cite{RefG04} introduced that $\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}\quad \boldsymbol{X}) = \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X})\oplus \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}\boldsymbol{Z})$. 
This shows that the necessary and sufficient condition \eqref{oplus} can be written as 
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{y} \in \left\{\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X})\ \cap \ \mathcal{N}[(\boldsymbol{K}_2 \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^\top)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}]\right\}  \oplus \mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{\Omega Z}).   
\end{align*}
\end{rem}
Additionally, if $\boldsymbol{K}_1$ equals $\boldsymbol{K}_2$, Theorem~\ref{thm52} can be restated as follows:
\begin{cor}\label{cor53}
Let $\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^{+}(k)$ and $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ be expressed as $\boldsymbol{\Omega} = \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\boldsymbol{X}^\top + \boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\Delta} \boldsymbol{Z}^\top$.
Then the equality \eqref{B2Ey} holds if and only if 
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{y} \in \left\{\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X})\ \cap \ \mathcal{N}[\boldsymbol{X}^\top(\boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{I}_n)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+ \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}]\right\}  \oplus \mathcal{C}^\bot(\boldsymbol{X}).
\end{align*}
\end{cor}
\begin{rem}
Let $\lambda$ be a positive constant and $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ be expressed as $\boldsymbol{\Omega} = \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\boldsymbol{X}^\top + \boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\Delta} \boldsymbol{Z}^\top$. 
Corollary~\ref{cor53} implies that the equality between ordinary ridge estimators
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \lambda^{-1}\boldsymbol{I}_k) = \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n, \lambda^{-1}\boldsymbol{I}_k) 
\end{align*}
holds if and only if 
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{y} \in \left\{\mathcal{C}(\boldsymbol{X})\ \cap \ \mathcal{N}[\boldsymbol{X}^\top(\boldsymbol{\Omega} - \boldsymbol{I}_n)(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\lambda^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{X}^\top)^{-1}]\right\}  \oplus \mathcal{C}^\bot(\boldsymbol{X}).
\end{align*}
\end{rem}
\section{Equality between residual sums of squares}\label{sec:6}
In this section, we derive equivalent conditions for the residual sums of squares.
First, we derive a necessary and sufficient condition for \eqref{BRSE1}.
To simplify the notation, let us denote
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{S} = (\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}, \quad \boldsymbol{T}= (\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}.
\end{align*}
The following theorem is a necessary and sufficient condition for \eqref{BRSE1}.
\begin{thm}\label{thm61}
Let $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ be expressed in \eqref{Orep}.
For given $\boldsymbol{K}_1, \boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$, the equality \eqref{BRSE1} holds if and only if both of the following conditions hold: 
\begin{align}
& \boldsymbol{\Omega}= \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}+\boldsymbol{Z}(\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top},\label{61}\\
& (\boldsymbol{\Gamma} +\boldsymbol{K}_1)\boldsymbol{\Gamma} ^{-1} (\boldsymbol{\Gamma} +\boldsymbol{K}_1)=\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}.\label{62}
\end{align}
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
For simplicity, $\boldsymbol{A}$ is defined as \eqref{ABCD}. 
Since
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1) 
&= \boldsymbol{y}-(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}-\boldsymbol{\Omega})(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}\\
&= \boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{y}+\boldsymbol{\Omega}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}\\
&=\boldsymbol{\Omega}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}=\boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{S}\boldsymbol{y}
\end{align*}
and 
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2) 
=(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})^{-1}\boldsymbol{y}=\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{y},
\end{align*}
the residual sums of squares $RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1)$ and $RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)$ can be rewritten as
\begin{align*}
&RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1)=\left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1)\right)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1)\right)=\boldsymbol{y}^{\top}\boldsymbol{S}\boldsymbol{\Omega}\boldsymbol{S}\boldsymbol{y}
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
&RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)=\left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)\right)^{\top} \left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)\right)=\boldsymbol{y}^{\top}\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{T} \boldsymbol{y} ,
\end{align*}
respectively. 
Thus, the problem is to derive a condition under which $\boldsymbol{y}^\top (\boldsymbol{S}\boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{S}-\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{T}  )\boldsymbol{y}=0$ holds for any $\boldsymbol{y}\in \mathbb{R}^{n}$, which is equivalent to deriving a condition for
\begin{align}\label{RSSK}
 &\boldsymbol{S}\boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{S}-\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{T}=\boldsymbol{0} \notag \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}=\boldsymbol{0},
\end{align}
since $\boldsymbol{S}$ and $\boldsymbol{T}$ are nonsingular.
The equality \eqref{RSSK}  holds if and only if the following three equalities simultaneously hold:
\begin{align}
\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}  ) \boldsymbol{X} =\boldsymbol{0}, \label{XXK} \\
\boldsymbol{X}^\top (\boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}  )  \boldsymbol{Z} =\boldsymbol{0}, \label{XZK} \\
\boldsymbol{Z}^\top (\boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}  ) \boldsymbol{Z} =\boldsymbol{0}. \label{ZZK}
\end{align}
The quantities $\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}$,  and $\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}$ are calculated as follows:
\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}\\
&= \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})\boldsymbol{X}\\
&= \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega} \boldsymbol{X} + 2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\\
&= \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}  + 2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X},
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z} \\
&= \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})\boldsymbol{Z}\\
&= \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega}\boldsymbol{Z} + 2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{Z} + \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Z}\\
&= \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\Xi}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z},
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z} \\
&= \boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})\boldsymbol{Z}\\
&= \boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega}\boldsymbol{Z} + 2\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{Z} + \boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Z}\\
&= \boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z} \boldsymbol{\Delta}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z},
\end{align*}
under $\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{Z}=\boldsymbol{0}$.
A straightforward calculation yields that
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}\boldsymbol{T}^{-1} 
=(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})(\boldsymbol{I}_n+\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top})
=\boldsymbol{I}_n + 2\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top + \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top.
\end{align*}
The equality \eqref{XZK} can be rewritten as 
\begin{align}\label{XZK2}
&\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{\Xi}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z} =\boldsymbol{0} \notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{\Xi} =\boldsymbol{0}
\end{align}
and \eqref{ZZK} is equivalent to 
\begin{align}\label{ZZK2}
&\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z} \boldsymbol{\Delta}\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z} =\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z} \notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{\Delta} =(\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}.
\end{align}
From \eqref{XZK2}, $\boldsymbol{A}$ becomes $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}^{-1}$. 
Hence, the equality \eqref{XXK} is equivalent to
\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}  + 2\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1 \boldsymbol{\Gamma}^{-1}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X} \\
&= \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} + 2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X} + \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{\Gamma} + 2\boldsymbol{K}_1 + \boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{\Gamma}^{-1}\boldsymbol{K}_1 = (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} + 2\boldsymbol{K}_2 + \boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & (\boldsymbol{\Gamma} +\boldsymbol{K}_1)\boldsymbol{\Gamma} ^{-1} (\boldsymbol{\Gamma} +\boldsymbol{K}_1)=\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X}\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}+\boldsymbol{K}_2\right\}.
\end{align*}
In addition, from \eqref{XZK2} and \eqref{ZZK2}, the covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ is of the following form:
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\Omega}= \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}+\boldsymbol{Z}(\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}.
\end{align*}
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
As stated in Section~\ref{sec:1}, \cite{RefMT25} derived an equivalence condition such that the equality between two residual sums of squares holds when general ridge estimators are considered.
Comparing their results with Theorem~\ref{thm61} provides us with further insights.
\begin{rem}
\cite{RefMT25} proved that the equality \eqref{RSSG} holds if and only if the following three conditions simultaneously hold in \eqref{Orep}:
\begin{align}
& \boldsymbol{K}_1 (\boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{K}_1)^{-1}\boldsymbol{A}(\boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{K}_1)^{-1}\boldsymbol{K}_1 = \boldsymbol{K}_2 (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{K}_2)^{-1}\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{K}_2)^{-1}\boldsymbol{K}_2,\notag\\
&\boldsymbol{K}_2 (\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{K}_2)^{-1}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{\Xi}=\boldsymbol{0}, \label{rss2}\\
&\boldsymbol{\Delta} = (\boldsymbol{Z}^\top \boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}, \label{rss3}
\end{align} 
where $\boldsymbol{A} = (\boldsymbol{\Gamma}-\boldsymbol{\Xi}\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Xi}^\top)^{-1}$, and $\boldsymbol{K}_1,\boldsymbol{K}_2\in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$ are fixed.
It can be seen from \eqref{61} and \eqref{rss3} that the results of Theorem~\ref{thm61} and theirs include
the condition $\boldsymbol{\Delta}=(\boldsymbol{Z}^\top\boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}$.
On the other hand, we find that
\eqref{rss2} is a necessary condition on $\boldsymbol{\Xi}$ for \eqref{61}.
\end{rem}
Let us demonstrate an example that satisfies \eqref{BRSE1}.
\begin{exam}[example for Theorem~\ref{thm61}]\label{Ex2}
Let
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{X}=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1 \\
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix}, \
\boldsymbol{Z}=
\begin{pmatrix}
 0 \\
 0 \\
 1
\end{pmatrix},
\
\boldsymbol{\Omega} =
\begin{pmatrix}
7+4\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1 
\end{pmatrix}, \ 
\boldsymbol{K}_1=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix},
\ \mbox{and}\ \,
\boldsymbol{K}_2= \begin{pmatrix}
3 & 0 \\
0 & 0
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
If we suppose that 
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\Gamma} = \begin{pmatrix}
7+4\sqrt{3} & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}, \ \boldsymbol{\Xi}=\boldsymbol{0},\ \mbox{and} \ \boldsymbol{\Delta}= 1,
\end{align*}
the covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ can be expressed as follows:
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\Omega}
=\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1 \\
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}
7+4\sqrt{3} & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1 \\
0 & 0
\end{pmatrix}^\top
+\begin{pmatrix}
 0 \\
 0  \\
 1
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
 0 \\
 0  \\
 1 
\end{pmatrix}^\top
=\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}+\boldsymbol{Z}(\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top},
\end{align*}
which implies that \eqref{61} is satisfied.
The left-hand side and right-hand side of \eqref{62}
both become 
$\begin{pmatrix}
16 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}$, and thus coincide.
From the above, the equality
\begin{align*}
RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K}_1)=RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_3,\boldsymbol{K}_2)\left(=\boldsymbol{y}^{\top}
\begin{pmatrix}
1/16 & 0 & 0\\
0& 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}\boldsymbol{y}\right) 
\end{align*}
holds for any $\boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^3$.   
\end{exam}
Hereafter, we consider the case where $\boldsymbol{K}_1 = \boldsymbol{K}_2 = \boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$ and discuss Theorem~\ref{thm61}.
Corollary~\ref{cor62} can be immediately proved by using Proposition~\ref{Pr23} and the result from \cite{RefMT25} (Corollary 4.3).  
\begin{cor}\label{cor62}
Let $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ be expressed in \eqref{Orep}.
For given $\boldsymbol{K} \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, 
\begin{align*}
RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}, \boldsymbol{K})=RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}) \ \mbox{for any} \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n    
\end{align*}
holds if and only if both of the following conditions hold:
\begin{align*}
& \boldsymbol{\Omega}= \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}+\boldsymbol{Z}(\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top},\\
& \boldsymbol{K}(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X})\boldsymbol{K}=(\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}-\boldsymbol{\Gamma}.
\end{align*}
\end{cor}
\begin{rem}
Let $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ be expressed in \eqref{Orep}.
If $\boldsymbol{K}= \lambda^{-1} \boldsymbol{I}_k \in \mathcal{S}^+(k)$, then Corollary~\ref{cor62} implies that $RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\lambda^{-1} \boldsymbol{I}_k)=RSS_{BL}(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\lambda^{-1} \boldsymbol{I}_k) \ \mbox{for any} \ \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ holds if and only if both of the following conditions hold:
\begin{align*}
    & \boldsymbol{\Omega}= \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{\Gamma} \boldsymbol{X}^{\top}+\boldsymbol{Z}(\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Z})^{-1}\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top},\\
    & \boldsymbol{\Gamma}^{-1}-\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X} =\lambda^2\left\{(\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{X})^{-1}-\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\right\}.
\end{align*}
\end{rem}
Next, we derive the necessary and sufficient condition for \eqref{B2E} and \eqref{BRSE1}.
\begin{thm}\label{thm63}
For given $\boldsymbol{K}_1, \boldsymbol{K}_2 \in \mathcal{S}^N(k)$, the two equalities \eqref{B2E} and \eqref{BRSE1} both hold if and only if the following two conditions
\begin{align*}
 \boldsymbol{K}_1 = \boldsymbol{K}_2 \quad \an \quad \boldsymbol{\Omega} = \boldsymbol{I}_n
\end{align*}
hold.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
First, by using \eqref{B2E}, \eqref{BRSE1} is equivalent to
\begin{align}
&\left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1)\right)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{BL}(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\boldsymbol{K}_1)\right) = \left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{{\beta}}}_B(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)\right)^\top \left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{{\beta}}}_B(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)\right) \notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{{\beta}}}_B(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)\right)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{{\beta}}}_B(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)\right) = \left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{{\beta}}}_B(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)\right)^\top \left(\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{X}\hat{\boldsymbol{{\beta}}}_B(\boldsymbol{I}_n,\boldsymbol{K}_2)\right) \notag\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &\boldsymbol{y}^\top \boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{T} \boldsymbol{y} = \boldsymbol{y}^\top\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{y} \label{OI}
\end{align}
for any $\boldsymbol{y}\in \mathbb{R}^n$. Moreover, \eqref{OI} is further equivalent to
\begin{align}\label{OEI}
&\boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1}\boldsymbol{T} = \boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{T}\notag \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad &\boldsymbol{\Omega} = \boldsymbol{I}_n,
\end{align}
since $\boldsymbol{T}$ is nonsingular.
On the other hand, \eqref{B2E} holds if and only if \eqref{4T} that can be rewritten as
\begin{align*}
&\boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top = \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_2\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}^\top  = \boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{K}_1\boldsymbol{X}^\top \boldsymbol{X}\\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \boldsymbol{K}_1 =\boldsymbol{K}_2
\end{align*}
since \eqref{OEI} and $\boldsymbol{X}^\top\boldsymbol{Z}=\boldsymbol{0}$ are satisfied. 
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\section{Concluding remarks}\label{sec:7}
In this paper, we revisited linear statistical
inference with Bayes linear estimators.
We provided some optimality results for the Bayes linear estimators, such as linear sufficiency, linear completeness, and the equivalent conditions under which two Bayes linear estimators coincide.
Additionally, we obtained several results regarding the equivalence of the residual sums of squares.

For future directions, it is interesting from the perspective of linear inference theory to study the case where $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ is not necessarily positive definite and $\boldsymbol{X}$ is not of full-rank.
