\section{Introduction}

Designing materials and structures for impact mitigation and vibration isolation requires efficient energy absorption. Architected materials that exploit geometric instabilities, such as snap-through buckling, enable reversible energy trapping through large elastic deformations \citep{Shan2015MultistableEnergy, Meza2014StrongNanolattices, Chen2021ReusableColumns, Bertoldi2017HarnessingMaterials, Yang2019Multi-stableInstability, Haghpanah2016MultistableMaterials, Frenzel2016TailoredAbsorbers}. Snap-through instability involves a rapid transition between two elastic equilibria, resulting in simultaneous shape change and unloading \citep{Qiu2004AMechanism}. The structure stores elastic strain energy in the buckled state until additional energy is applied to restore the original configuration. However, energy absorption in purely elastic bistable structures is rate-independent, limiting their effectiveness under dynamic or impact loading.

To overcome this limitation, recent studies have incorporated rate-dependent dissipation mechanisms, such as viscoelasticity, into bistable systems \citep{Jeon2022SynergisticElastomersb, Liu2024Rate-dependentMetamaterials, Li2022HarnessingMaterials, Valizadeh2024Rate-dependentPhotopolymerization, Yao2024TunableRates, Liu2022BucklingShells}. Among viscoelastic materials, liquid crystal elastomers (LCEs) are particularly promising due to the coupling between polymer network deformation and mesogen rotation. LCEs consist of crosslinked polymer networks embedded with stiff, rod-like mesogens aligned along a preferred direction (the director). This coupling gives rise to reversible actuation \citep{Warner2003InternationalEditors, Kuupfer1994LiquidProcesses}, soft stress behavior \citep{Azoug2016ViscoelasticityElastomers, MartinLinares2020TheElastomer}, and enhanced dissipation \citep{Clarke2001SoftElastomers, Merkel2019MechanicalLoading}. Compared to conventional elastomers, LCEs exhibit elevated loss factors over a broad frequency and temperature range and pronounced hysteresis under cyclic loading, driven by both polymer network viscoelasticity and mesogen reorientation~\citep{Azoug2016ViscoelasticityElastomers, MartinLinares2020TheElastomer, Clarke2001SoftElastomers, Merkel2019MechanicalLoading, Traugutt2020Liquid-Crystal-Elastomer-BasedPrinting, shen2025dissertation}.

Soft stress arises when mesogens rotate under loading, typically in polydomain or monodomain LCEs with directors initially perpendicular to the applied strain \citep{Azoug2016ViscoelasticityElastomers, MartinLinares2020TheElastomer, Merkel2018ThermomechanicalElastomers, Clarke1998TextureElastomers, Takebe2020SupersoftTests, Mistry2021SoftElastomers}. The stress–strain response exhibits an initial linear regime up to peak stress, followed by a softening plateau due to viscous mesogen rotation and a stiffening regime associated with chain alignment. These features are often accompanied by significant hysteresis and inelastic strain upon unloading, making soft stress a powerful dissipation mechanism at finite strain rates.

In previous work, we developed architected lattices composed of repeating unit cells with tilted LCE beams sandwiched between rigid horizontal supports \citep{Jeon2022SynergisticElastomersb}. These structures combined snap-through buckling and LCE viscoelasticity to enhance energy absorption. While they exhibited snap-through transitions, the buckled shape was maintained for an extended period and gradually recovered due to viscoelastic relaxation. During this time, the reaction force remained nonzero, indicating pseudo-bistability \citep{Brinkmeyer2013Pseudo-bistablePanels, Gomez2019DynamicsSnap-through, Che2019Temperature-tunableInstabilities, Chen2022SpatiotemporallySnapping}. However, strains in the tilted beams during buckling remained below 30–40\%, insufficient to activate soft stress via mesogen rotation. Because this rotation is largely irreversible, its activation offers potential for significantly greater energy dissipation. Mistry et al.~\citep{Mistry2021SoftElastomers} reported plateau-like soft stress enabling impact absorption at nearly constant stress, and Shaha et al.~\citep{Shaha2020BiocompatibleDisc} observed stronger soft stress under transverse tension than longitudinal compression, likely due to increased mesogen reorientation.

Despite this promise, the use of soft stress for structural-scale dissipation remains largely unexplored. Luo et al.~\citep{Luo20213DInsultb} indirectly investigated this mechanism by showing that increased lattice connectivity ($Z$) in polydomain LCE foams enhanced energy dissipation, which they attributed to greater axial strain and possible mesogen rotation, though this was not directly measured. Related studies on bi-material lattices incorporated rigid inclined struts connected to highly extensible transverse elements \citep{Ye2023MultimaterialMetamaterials, Ruschel2020ALattices}. These systems absorb energy through axial stretching of the transverse strut under vertical compression while suppressing buckling in the inclined beams. Moreover, the materials used in these studies were conventional elastomers, such as thermoplastic polyurethane (TPU), rather than LCEs. As a result, they did not exploit the soft stress mechanism, which is unique to LCEs and enables energy dissipation through mesogen rotation, nor did they combine it with viscoelastic bending.

Here, we introduce tension-induced soft stress in LCEs as an active dissipation mechanism in bistable LCE structures subjected to macroscopic compression. Building on our previous design \citep{Jeon2022SynergisticElastomersb}, we replace the rigid horizontal member with a stretchable LCE bar. Under compression, the tilted beams bend while the horizontal bar stretches, activating soft stress through mesogen rotation. However, lateral expansion of the stretched bar reduces the tilt angle, diminishing bending and its associated dissipation. This tradeoff creates a nonlinear interaction between viscoelastic bending and soft-stress-driven stretching. We systematically investigate this interplay using finite element simulations and experiments, varying LCE material properties and the thickness ratio between horizontal and tilted members. A nonlinear viscoelastic user-defined element (UEL) was implemented in Abaqus/Standard to capture finite strain behavior, including soft stress from mesogen rotation. Energy contributions from mesogen rotation and polymer network viscoelasticity were quantitatively assessed. Our results reveal a non-monotonic dependence of energy dissipation on the thickness ratio, with maximum absorption achieved at an intermediate value. Optimized structures dissipated two to three times more energy than those with rigid bars, demonstrating the synergistic benefit of combining viscoelastic bending and tension-induced soft stress.




