\section{Introduction}

Scientific simulation powered by high-performance computing (HPC) enables researchers to model complex natural systems at unprecedented scale and fidelity. It allows for accurate predictions and insights in fields like climate science, physics, and biology, where real-world experimentation is costly, slow, or impossible \cite{cappello2025lossy, rosa2020data, di2024survey}. 
For example, cosmological simulations based on Dark Matter Evolution and Self-Gravity Equations~\cite{almgren2013nyx, sexton2021nyx} leverage HPC to model the Lyman-$\alpha$ forest, baryonic matter distribution, and thermodynamics of the intergalactic medium (IGM), offering more profound insights into early-universe astrophysics~\cite{lukic2015lyman, onorbe2019inhomogeneous}. However, to accelerate scientific discovery and inform critical decisions, these applications routinely generate petabyte- to exabyte-scale data as model complexities grow. For instance, the CMIP climate project~\cite{meehl2000coupled} produced 2.5 PB of data, and cutting-edge fluid dynamics~\cite{johnson2023exploring} runs can yield hundreds of terabytes per simulation. Storing and accessing such massive data on existing HPC systems can overwhelm shared I/O resources, degrading disk performance and impacting other users' read/write operations~\cite{kim2023design, behzad2019optimizing, paul2020understanding}. Worse yet, transferring these data across systems or storage tiers can saturate network bandwidth, leading to contention and delays~\cite{kang2022study, chunduri2019gpcnet}. These I/O and communication bottlenecks highlight the need to reduce source data volume. Efficient data compression has thus become crucial for managing and scaling with the realities of modern scientific data generation on HPC systems.

Early efforts in scientific data compression focused on lossless techniques~\cite{ziv1977universal, GZIP1996, lindstrom2006fast, gailly2004zlib}, which typically achieve limited ratios (1$\times$–3$\times$) on scientific data~\cite{zhao2020sdrbench}. In contrast, lossy compression methods~\cite{lindstrom2014fixed, di2016sz, liu2022qoz, zhao2021splinesz, tao2017significant, liang2019hybrid, tao2019optimizing, liang2022sz3} offer much higher ratios -- for example, up to 436$\times$ with SZ~\cite{di2016sz} -- while allowing slight fidelity loss within user-defined error tolerances, which indeed alleviates storage bottlenecks and reduces bandwidth. Among its core components, predictors, such as curve fitting~\cite{di2016sz} and spline interpolation~\cite{lakshminarasimhan2013isabela}, play a crucial role in exploiting local data correlations. 
However, these methods use \emph{pre-determined}, \emph{closed-form} approximation, which often fails to capture complex or irregular features in scientific data. As a result, they may overlook latent correlations and ultimately degrade compression efficiency. 
Deep neural networks (DNNs), known as powerful predictors~\cite{hornik1989multilayer}, have shown strong potential in lossy compression ~\cite{liu2021autoencoder, liu2023srnsz, liu2024scw, li2024attention, gwlz, jia2024neurlz}. By enhancing hand-crafted predictors, DNNs better handle irregular data features and, therefore, improve compression efficacy. As a result, combining traditional compressors with learning-based models has become a promising trend for high-ratio, error-bounded scientific data reduction.

Neural-hybrid lossy compression integrating DNNs offers impressive compression ratios and reconstruction quality, but introduces significant challenges in computational cost and data movement across multiple general-purpose hardware systems. \Circled{1} \textbf{CPU-only} systems
% , limited by insufficient computational power (e.g., parallelism and concurrency \cite{CPU-HBM-not-working}), 
generally struggle to support the high data volumes required for efficient processing: the typical throughput of CPU-based compressors is at an order of magnitude of $10^{-1}$ gigabytes/sec~\cite{liu2024cusz}, 
% falling short of 
far below
the data rates generated by modern simulators and sensors~\cite{tian2020cusz}.
Furthermore, %with neural-processing in mind, 
% the limited parallelism in CPUs becomes a major bottleneck for neural components that require high-parallelism operations, such as convolutions and matrix multiplications. 
the limited \emph{parallelism} and \emph{concurrency} \cite{CPU-HBM-not-working} cap the neural capabilities, such as convolutions and matrix multiplications.
% Despite multi-core CPUs and SIMD extensions providing some acceleration, they fall short of the performance achievable by GPUs. 
\Circled{2} \textbf{GPU-only} systems, equipped with thousands of parallel cores, can efficiently execute neural operations and demonstrate throughput improvements ranging from $10\times$ to $100\times$ over CPUs. However, GPU efficiency is often compromised by non-neural components, such as interpolation and lossless encoding, which are sequential or control-flow intensive, leading to underutilization of GPU resources. Additionally, frequent kernel launches and the memory-bound nature of non-neural operations exacerbate this inefficiency. \Circled{3} To address the limitations of single hardware platforms, \textbf{CPU-GPU} pipelines have been proposed. However, these hybrid systems suffer from significant data transfer overheads via PCIe, especially when neural inference is performed on the GPU and subsequent data reconstruction occurs on the CPU. This overhead leads to considerable performance degradation, as the benefits of GPU acceleration are often negated by the latency induced by data movement. Consequently, hybrid architectures often fail to meet performance expectations, which has sometimes prompted the design of customized hardware platforms~\cite{huang2024cuszp2}. 
% In summary, each platform presents distinct trade-offs: (1) \textbf{CPU-Only} systems lack the necessary parallelism for real-time neural compression; (2) \textbf{GPU-Only} systems provide high computational throughput but require careful optimization to avoid sequential bottlenecks and inefficiencies; and (3) \textbf{CPU-GPU} systems necessitate tightly coordinated scheduling and minimized data movement to remain viable. Overcoming these challenges is essential for transitioning neural-enhanced lossy compression from research prototypes to practical, high-throughput deployment.

While an application-specific ASIC appears to be a natural solution to the limitations of general-purpose processors, a naive implementation of neural-hybrid lossy compression faces significant challenges. \ul{First}, the level-wise predictor introduces a memory-bound bottleneck due to irregular access patterns and extensive intermediate data that saturate on-chip buffers and exert excessive pressure on off-chip bandwidth. \ul{Second}, the absence of inter-stage pipelining leads to severe idle cycles, as each module -- predictor, neural network, and lossless codec -- must wait for its predecessor to finish before commencing execution. \ul{Third}, fixed-function datapaths hinder scalability, making it difficult to adapt to varying data sizes and workloads without costly hardware redesign.


