\section{Background and Motivation}

\subsection{Neural-Hybrid Lossy Compression}


The state-of-the-art neural-hybrid lossy compressor, NeurLZ~\cite{jia2024neurlz}, adopts a hybrid design that preserves compatibility with conventional compression pipelines while selectively integrating neural modules to enhance reconstruction accuracy, based on the SZ3~\cite{liang2022sz3} algorithm. As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:neurlzOverview}, NeurLZ integrates conventional non-neural components (i.e., interpolation-based prediction, quantization, and lossless encoding/decoding), with lightweight neural operators that are trained online to enhance prediction accuracy.

During compression, the input data is initially processed by a conventional lossy compressor that employs level-wise, block-based iterative interpolation, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:originalComputationOrder}. At each level, the reconstructed data from the previous level are partitioned into fixed-size blocks, where an interpolation module predicts new data values based on neighboring values generated earlier along each data dimension. The resulting prediction error, defined as the difference between the original and interpolated values, can be significant and is subsequently quantized within a user-specified error bound. This quantization step produces two outputs: (1) the quantization code, which is passed to a lossless compressor for entropy encoding and forms part of the final compressed bitstream, and (2) the reconstructed value, derived from the interpolation prediction and the quantization code, which serves as the reference for predicting data in subsequent levels, thereby preserving continuity and enhancing prediction accuracy. After all reconstructed data are generated by the conventional compressor, they are globally normalized and passed to a lightweight neural network module for online training. This neural module extracts additional features that are appended to the output stream, enriching the learned representation.



The procedure operates in reverse order during decompression. It begins by decoding the quantization codes from the bitstream using a lossless decoder, followed by reconstructing each data point via iterative interpolation, leveraging previously reconstructed values as reference. After completing the full reconstruction, a global normalization step is applied, and the normalized data is further refined through a lightweight neural network whose parameters are retrieved from the bitstream to enhance final reconstruction quality.

\begin{comment}
NeurLZ adopts a hybrid design that preserves compatibility with conventional compression pipelines while selectively integrating neural modules to enhance reconstruction accuracy. 

Figure~\ref{fig:neurlzOverview} presents an overview of NeurLZ's compression and decompression runtime. The system combines conventional non-neural components (e.g., interpolation, quantization, and lossless compression) with lightweight neural operators that are trained online during runtime.

During compression, the input data is first processed by a conventional lossy compressor in an iterative manner. For each data point, an interpolation module predicts its value based on the reconstructed values of its preceding neighboring points. The resulting prediction error, potentially large, is then quantized within a user-defined error bound in the quantization module. This quantization step yields two outputs: the quantization code is forwarded to a lossless compressor for entropy encoding, contributing to the final output bitstream; simultaneously, the reconstructed value is generated and reused in subsequent interpolation steps to maintain continuity and prediction accuracy.
Once all reconstructed data have been generated by the conventional pipeline, they are globally normalized and then fed into a lightweight neural network for online learning. This module extracts features that are also appended to the output stream, augmenting the representation learned by the conventional compressor.

During decompression, the conventional decompressor also operates iteratively. It first decodes the quantization codes from the bitstream using the lossless decoder, then reconstructs each data point via interpolation based on previously reconstructed values. Once all data have been reconstructed, a global normalization is applied, followed by enhancement through the inference of the neural network loaded from the bitstream.
